 0.007 EFLOP; WikiPPL 2048x512 = 0.027 EFLOP; C4 11kx4096 = 1.14 EFLOP\\n  70B : calib fwd 128x2048 = 0.041 EFLOP; WikiPPL 2048x512 = 0.163 EFLOP; C4 11kx4096 = 7.02 EFLOP\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "d77e20f6-fa12-47c8-908c-19f6200ec57f", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "496ff686-0c86-403b-b630-624731f84598", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:40:40.144684", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c09e22cd-ca29-426c-8e2f-8b4d93429669", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Good. I have analytic outputs. Note these are analytic arithmetic, not measurements. I should be careful: KV share numbers, roofline ridge points etc. — these are derived from datasheet specs which are assumptions (A100 2.039 TB/s, 312 TFLOPS FP16 TC, H100 3.35 TB/s, 989 TFLOPS FP16 dense). Mark as assumed vendor specs.\n\nKey analytic findings worth reporting:\n1. W4 weights with group-128 scale overhead → 0.531 B/elem vs 2.0 → weight-byte compression ratio 3.76x (not 4x).\n2. Decode traffic ratio (fp16 vs W4A4) is 3.6–3.9x across all (B,S) for 7B/13B — so decode *should* be near 3.5x if bandwidth-limited and kernel efficient. At B=1 KV share is tiny (<1%), so KV quant barely matters for decode traffic at B=1; it matters for *capacity*/max batch, not speed. At B=64,S=4096, KV is 91% of traffic for 7B → KV quant dominates there. This is a crucial insight for the design: KV quantization's performance effect is a function of B*S, and it should be tested at large B*S, not at B=1. Excellent testable prediction.\n3. 70B with GQA has small KV/token (320KB) → KV quant gives little traffic benefit even at B=64 (35%). So KV-quant ablation (A5 vs A3) will show near-zero decode effect at 70B but big at 7B/13B. Great hypothesis to state as prior.\n4. Roofline crossover M*: FP16 ridge at AI 153 flop/B (A100), memory→compute crossover at ~153 tokens/weight-column... my M* computation: tokens per GEMM row-block. Meaning: GEMMs with M (tokens) below ~153 are memory-bound on A100 FP16; below ~81 for W4 (since weights smaller → need fewer tokens to become compute-bound). So decode (M=batch) is memory-bound unless B>153 → decode at B≤64 remains memory-bound in both arms. Prefill with S*B ≥ 1024 is compute-bound in both arms. This is the analytic justification for the regime definition. Good.\n5. Compute-bound ceiling for W4A4 = 2x over FP16 on A100 (INT4 TC 2x INT8 = 4x? Actually A100 INT8 = 624 TOPS = 2x FP16 312; INT4 = 1248 TOPS = 4x). Hmm I set int4=624 which is wrong for A100 — A100 INT4 is 1248 TOPS (with sparsity 2x more). Let me note: A100 INT8 624 TOPS, INT4 1248 TOPS. So compute-bound ceiling would be 4x on paper. But H100 dropped INT4 TC (1979 TOPS INT8, INT4 emulated as 2xINT8 = same effective). I set int4=624 for A100, giving 2.0x — that's conservative (equal to INT8). I should present the ceiling as a parameter range 2x–4x depending on whether INT4 TC path is actually used, and note that the analytic ceiling on H100 is ~1x relative to FP16 (1979/989 = 2x for INT8 → 2x). Let me just recompute correctly and present both.\n\nActually, let me be careful and just present the formula and both parameterizations rather than a single number, and flag vendor-spec numbers as assumptions.\n\n6. Online Hadamard per-token overhead is tiny in FLOPs (0.02–0.07% of layer FLOPs) and tiny in traffic (16–112 KB/token) per decode step → predicted decode cost negligible if fused; the risk is *unfused* extra kernel launches (32 layers × 2–4 extra kernels × launch latency ~5µs = 0.3–0.6 ms/step, which at a target decode step of a few ms is 10-20%+). That's a launch-overhead/occupancy effect, not an arithmetic one — exactly the kernel-maturity confound. Good, analytic prediction: launch-overhead-bound, measurable via CUDA graph toggle.\n7. KV-cache full refresh Hadamard (if implemented as periodic full-cache re-rotation) scales as S·log S → at 7B the ratio vs a decode step reaches ~1.0 at S=4096 and 8x at 32k → predicted crossover around S≈4k for 7B, 16k for 70B. That's a beautiful pre-registered prediction to test on the long-context ladder. (Caveat: QuaRot's actual scheme applies Hadamard at write-time per-token, or every n tokens, so the refresh cost may be amortized differently — flag as assumption about implementation.)\n\nNow let me finalize GPU-hour budget: my estimate is rough. I'll present budget with placeholder utilization 0.4 and note it's analytic. Let me recompute a cleaner budget including 3 seeds and quant + PPL costs.\n\nLet me do a final aggregation calc for the budget table.\n\nActually, the numbers I got for per-arm GPU hours look small (0.41 GPUh for 7B perf sweep), that's fine since prefill FLOPs at 30 reps dominate. Let me quickly compute the total design budget: arms × models × seeds, plus quantization offline cost, plus PPL eval.\n\nQuant cost: 7B calibration 0.003 EFLOP = 3 TFLOP... wait 0.003 EFLOP = 3e15 FLOP. At 312e12 * 0.4 = 1.25e14 per sec → 24 s. Hmm that seems small; 128 seqs × 2048 tokens × 2 × 6.48e9 = 3.4e15 → 27 seconds at 40% MFU. Reasonable (activation statistics gathering is more expensive due to memory but ok).\n\nC4 PPL for 7B: 0.58 EFLOP → 0.58e18/1.25e14 = 4640 s = 1.29 h. Hmm, 11000 seqs × 4096 tokens is the full C4 val set; QuaRot/GPTQ-style often uses a subset. I'll mark C4 as configurable subset (e.g., 256×4096) to control cost, and WikiText-2 full is the primary.\n\nLet me just compute a budget table quickly. I'll compute total GPU-hours for the whole matrix.\n\nLet me do one more computation to get a defensible budget, then write the final answer.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /tmp/dry && cat > /tmp/dry/c.py <<'PY'\n# ---- vendor spec ASSUMPTIONS (datasheet values, not measured) ----\nHW={\"A100-80G\":dict(bw=2.039e12,fp16=312e12,int8=624e12,int4=1248e12,int4tc=True),\n    \"H100-80G\":dict(bw=3.352e12,fp16=989e12,int8=1979e12,int4=2*1979e12,int4tc=False)}\nprint(\"== compute-bound W4 ceiling vs FP16, parameterized ==\")\nfor k,v in HW.items():\n    eff4 = v['int4'] if v['int4tc'] else 2*v['int8']/2   # no INT4 TC -> INT8-pair emulation\n    print(f\"  {k}: peak-int4-path={eff4/1e12:.0f} TOPS eff, FP16={v['fp16']/1e12:.0f} -> ceiling={eff4/v['fp16']:.1f}x ; int8-alt={v['int8']/v['fp16']:.1f}x\")\nprint()\nN={\"7B\":6.48e9,\"13B\":12.69e9,\"70B\":77.85e9}; V=32000; U=0.40; FP=312e12\nS=[128,512,1024,2048,4096]; B=[1,8,32,64]; NREP=30; DECODE={128:128,512:128,1024:128,2048:128,4096:256}\ndef perf_hours(n):\n    h=0\n    for s in S:\n        h+= 2*(N[n]+V)*s*64*NREP/(FP*U*3600)                  # prefill sweep (largest batch)\n        for b in B: h+= 2*(N[n]+V)*b*DECODE[s]*len(S)*2/(FP*U*3600)  # decode sweep (x2 for both arms)\n    return h\ndef quant_hours(n, calib=128*2048): return 2*N[n]*calib*3/(FP*U*3600)     # 3 fwd passes incl. scale search\ndef ppl_hours(n, seqs=2048, L=512):  return 2*N[n]*seqs*L/(FP*U*3600)*2   # Wiki-2 + C4-subset\nARMS={\"A0\":1,\"A1\":1,\"A2\":1,\"A3\":1,\"A4\":1,\"A5\":1,\"A6\":1,\"A7\":0}   # A7 W8A8 optional\nSEEDS=3\ntot=0\nfor n in [\"7B\",\"13B\",\"70B\"]:\n    a=sum(ARMS.values())\n    perf=a*perf_hours(n); q=a*quant_hours(n)/SEEDS; p=a*ppl_hours(n)\n    t=perf+q+p; tot+=t\n    print(f\"  {n:4}: perf={perf:6.2f}h  quant-offline={q:5.2f}h  ppl={p:6.2f}h  TOTAL={t:7.2f} GPUh (A100, MFU={U:.0%}, {a} arms, no 70B sharding penalty)\")\nprint(f\"  GRAND TOTAL (7B+13B+70B) ~ {tot:.1f} GPUh ; 7B+13B only ~ {tot-sum([0]):.1f}\")\nprint()\nprint(\"== predicted decode speedup ceiling = weight+KV traffic ratio (bandwidth-limited ideal) ==\")\nprint(\"   7B/13B: ~3.6-3.9x everywhere; 70B: ~3.7x but KV share only 0.9-35% -> KV-quant ablation nearly inert at 70B\")\nprint(\"== predicted launch-overhead sensitivity (unfused extra kernels) ==\")\nfor n,L in [(\"7B\",32),(\"13B\",40),(\"70B\",80)]:\n    for k in (2,4):\n        print(f\"   {n:4} {k} extra kernels/layer: {L*k*5/1000:.3f} ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\")\nPY\npython3 /tmp/dry/c.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bc25eec897284a9d", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-bc25eec897284a9d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /tmp/dry 