l_hours(n)\\n    t=perf+q+p; tot+=t\\n    print(f\\\"  {n:4}: perf={perf:6.2f}h  quant-offline={q:5.2f}h  ppl={p:6.2f}h  TOTAL={t:7.2f} GPUh (A100, MFU={U:.0%}, {a} arms, no 70B sharding penalty)\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"  GRAND TOTAL (7B+13B+70B) ~ {tot:.1f} GPUh ; 7B+13B only ~ {tot-sum([0]):.1f}\\\")\\nprint()\\nprint(\\\"== predicted decode speedup ceiling = weight+KV traffic ratio (bandwidth-limited ideal) ==\\\")\\nprint(\\\"   7B/13B: ~3.6-3.9x everywhere; 70B: ~3.7x but KV share only 0.9-35% -> KV-quant ablation nearly inert at 70B\\\")\\nprint(\\\"== predicted launch-overhead sensitivity (unfused extra kernels) ==\\\")\\nfor n,L in [(\\\"7B\\\",32),(\\\"13B\\\",40),(\\\"70B\\\",80)]:\\n    for k in (2,4):\\n        print(f\\\"   {n:4} {k} extra kernels/layer: {L*k*5/1000:.3f} ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\\")\\nPY\\npython3 /tmp/dry/c.py\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.115, \"output\": \"== compute-bound W4 ceiling vs FP16, parameterized ==\\n  A100-80G: peak-int4-path=1248 TOPS eff, FP16=312 -> ceiling=4.0x ; int8-alt=2.0x\\n  H100-80G: peak-int4-path=1979 TOPS eff, FP16=989 -> ceiling=2.0x ; int8-alt=2.0x\\n\\n  7B  : perf=  3.19h  quant-offline= 0.05h  ppl=  0.42h  TOTAL=   3.67 GPUh (A100, MFU=40%, 7 arms, no 70B sharding penalty)\\n  13B : perf=  6.25h  quant-offline= 0.10h  ppl=  0.83h  TOTAL=   7.18 GPUh (A100, MFU=40%, 7 arms, no 70B sharding penalty)\\n  70B : perf= 38.32h  quant-offline= 0.64h  ppl=  5.09h  TOTAL=  44.05 GPUh (A100, MFU=40%, 7 arms, no 70B sharding penalty)\\n  GRAND TOTAL (7B+13B+70B) ~ 54.9 GPUh ; 7B+13B only ~ 54.9\\n\\n== predicted decode speedup ceiling = weight+KV traffic ratio (bandwidth-limited ideal) ==\\n   7B/13B: ~3.6-3.9x everywhere; 70B: ~3.7x but KV share only 0.9-35% -> KV-quant ablation nearly inert at 70B\\n== predicted launch-overhead sensitivity (unfused extra kernels) ==\\n   7B   2 extra kernels/layer: 0.320 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n   7B   4 extra kernels/layer: 0.640 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n   13B  2 extra kernels/layer: 0.400 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n   13B  4 extra kernels/layer: 0.800 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n   70B  2 extra kernels/layer: 0.800 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n   70B  4 extra kernels/layer: 1.600 ms/step at 5us/launch (launch-bound, arithmetic-free)\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "496ff686-0c86-403b-b630-624731f84598", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "b644f973-05e3-44c8-9285-120b1c394330", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:44:03.300377", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "4eda7aee-132c-4cd6-bc40-08c3f344f066", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "I have all the analytic dry-run material. Now write the full deliverable design as stage=result. Keep it comprehensive but organized. No measured numbers; analytic numbers labeled as such with vendor-spec assumptions flagged.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n0. Status/scope note\n1. Rationale & causal model (what determines prefill vs decode delta)\n2. Fixed task requirements vs assumptions\n3. Control structure: arms table (with hypothesis + expected direction on prefill/decode), controls, seeds\n4. Config matrix\n5. Measurement protocol\n6. Analysis plan: regime framing, speedup-vs-AI, ΔPPL gates, decision rules / attribution rules\n7. Analytic dry-run results (CPU-feasible, done) — these are computations I actually did in Python (arithmetic), so they can be reported as \"computed here\" but labeled analytic, model constants assumed.\n8. Resources/runtime estimate with assumptions\n9. Risks/limitations\n10. Deliverable artifacts list\n11. Remaining assumptions/uncertainties\n\nBe careful: the analytic numbers use public LLaMA-2 config constants and vendor datasheet specs — both assumptions. State clearly. Also note the group-128 scale overhead model.\n\nAlso note the decode traffic ratio ~3.6-3.9x and that compute-bound ceiling on A100 = 4x (INT4 TC 1248 TOPS vs 312 TFLOPS), on H100 = 2x. These are ceilings, not predictions of measurement.\n\nNote the interesting finding: 70B has GQA → KV share small → KV-quant ablation nearly inert at 70B in throughput terms, but KV quantization's real benefit at 70B is *capacity* (fits on 1 GPU in 4-bit: 39 GB weights vs 145.5 GB). That's a footprint result worth calling out as an arm-level metric: feasibility/capacity, not just speed.\n\nAlso decode M* crossover: ~153 tokens for FP16 on A100, ~81 for W4 → decode at B≤64 is far below crossover → memory bound ✓; prefill at S·B ≥ 1024 far above → compute bound ✓. Good analytic justification of the regime definitions. Include that.\n\nAlso online Hadamard: per-token marginal cost is <0.1% of layer FLOPs, but launch overhead 0.3–1.6 ms/step if unfused → the design's prediction: Hadamard cost is a *plumbing* cost, not an arithmetic cost, hence fused/unfused toggle is decisive. And KV-refresh cost grows linearly in S: at S=32k refresh cost ~8x a decode step (7B) → crossover around S≈4k for 7B where refresh cost ≈ 1 decode step. That's the long-context ladder prediction to test. Note the caveat: refresh happens every N tokens / periodically, so the marginal cost per generated token depends on the refresh policy — parameterize.\n\nDecision rules:\n- Claim \"W4A4 helps decode but not prefill\" iff: decode B=1 speedup CI lower bound > 1.05 AND prefill large-batch speedup CI contains 1.0 or is <1.05, with quality gate passed.\n- Attribute to kernel immaturity if: matched-shape GEMM microbench achieves INT4:FP16 ratio below, say, 0.5× of the ISA ceiling ratio AND end-to-end prefill shortfall is accounted for (±20%) by the microbench deficit + measured Hadamard/quant op time. i.e., attribution residual.\n- Accounting identity: T_quant - T_fp16 = Σ(ΔGEMM) + T_rotate + T_quantize + T_resid. If |T_resid| < 20% of the gap → attribution accepted; else unexplained → report as unresolved.\n- Quality gate: ΔPPL thresholds.\n- \"Scheme-level win\" claim requires the win to survive the fused variant and be present in ≥2 of 3 seeds etc.\n\nAlso 70B drop criteria pre-registered: drop if 70B requires >2 GPUs, or if sharded arms exceed a wall-clock cap, or if the 70B arms' estimated cost > X% of budget (e.g., >50% of total GPU-hours, which analytic estimate says 44 of 55 GPUh = 80% → so likely drop). Nice: the analytic budget already shows 70B = 80% of cost → pre-register dropping 70B to throughput-only at 2 shapes, or keep for quality only. Good concrete recommendation.\n\nLet me write it up now. Keep organized and reasonably dense but readable. Use markdown tables.\n\nRegarding \"seeds\": 3 calibration seeds × 3 rotation seeds = 9 checkpoints per arm-model is expensive for PPL; propose factorized: 3 rotation seeds × 1 calibration seed for PPL, and note calibration-seed effect is tested only at 7B with 3 seeds (calibration seed affects scales). Good.\n\nTiming details: warmup, iters, medians, CUDA graphs, clock lock (nvidia-smi -lgc), MPS off, ECC on, no other processes, pinned order randomized, 3 process restarts, run-order randomization to avoid thermal drift confound.\n\nAlso: prefill measurement must exclude tokenization; TTFT defined as time from prefill kernel start to first sampled token (include lm_head + sample). State the operational definition.\n\nAlso: same attention backend (FlashAttention) across arms, since KV dtype changes attention kernel path; A5 keeps KV16, A3 uses 4-bit KV requiring dequant-in-attention → must use the same fused attention kernel or add an explicit control.\n\nAlso equal-quality-vs-equal-config reporting: report both iso-config speedup and iso-quality (only report speedup for arms passing the ΔPPL gate).\n\nNow write final answer. It should be detailed. Include the dry-run outputs as evidence.\n\nI'll include a compact version of key analytic tables in evidence.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# QuaRot W4A4 on LLaMA-2 — Experiment Design (prefill vs decode)\n\n**Status:** design-only. Nothing here is a measured result. All quantitative values below are either **analytic arithmetic** (computed in this sandbox, formulas shown) or **placeholders**. Facts that could not be verified in this environment are tagged **[ASSUMPTION]**.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Scope fixed by researcher approval\n\n| Item | Fixed decision |\n|---|---|\n| Hardware | **A100-80GB**, 1 GPU for 7B/13B. **H100 parameterized as the \"no-INT4-tensor-core\" alternative condition** `[A_alt]`; prefill conclusions are hardware-pinned and never pooled across the two. |\n| Models | LLaMA-2 **7B + 13B primary**; **70B optional**, needs ≥2×80 GB, with a pre-registered drop rule (§8). |\n| Quality | **Perplexity primary** (WikiText-2 len-512, C4 subset); zero-shot battery = secondary/diagnostic. Pre-registered ΔPPL band defines \"quality-neutral\". |\n| Headline | **Two regime-specific speedups**: decode = batch-1 per-token latency; prefill = large-batch (S≥1024, B≥8) tokens/s + TTFT. **No single \"average speedup\" is ever reported.** |\n| Published QuaRot numbers | Sanity **reference anchor only**, separate table, explicitly labelled *externally reported*, never inside our CIs. |\n| CPU dry run | Included; **arithmetic only** (FLOP/byte accounting, roofline regime map, bytes-moved/token, KV-bytes/token). No latency/PPL claim from it. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale — the causal model the design tests\n\nTwo different bottlenecks are in play, so a single scalar answer is meaningless:\n\n- **Decode at B=1** is **memory-bandwidth-bound**. The only thing that helps is moving fewer bytes: weights (2 B → ~0.53 B with group-128 scales) and KV cache (2 B → 0.5 B). Ceiling ≈ weight/KV byte-compression ratio, **independent of INT4 tensor cores**. QuaRot adds *new* per-token work (activation quantization, online Hadamards) that is FLOP-cheap but **traffic- and launch-heavy** → can eat the gain.\n- **Prefill at S·B ≥ 1024** is **compute-bound**. Gain ceiling = (INT4-path peak TOPS)/(FP16 peak), i.e. **hardware-pinned**: ~4× on A100 (INT4 TC) but only ~2× on H100 (no INT4 TC → INT8-pair path) **[ASSUMPTION: datasheet specs]**. Plus per-layer quantize/rotate overhead that FP16 does not pay.\n\nSo the design's central object is **speedup as a function of arithmetic intensity**, and the causal question is *which component (weight bytes, KV bytes, GEMM ISA, rotation, quantization) supplies the gain or the cost in each regime*.\n\n**Analytic regime justification (computed here, A100 datasheet values [ASSUMPTION]):** memory→compute crossover token-count M\\* = (peak/bandwidth)·bytes_per_elem/2 → **FP16 M\\* ≈ 153 tokens, W4 M\\* ≈ 81 tokens**. Decode at B ≤ 64 sits far below → bandwidth-bound ✔. Prefill at S·B ≥ 1024 sits far above → compute-bound ✔. These definitions are therefore not arbitrary.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Arms, each tied to a hypothesis with a pre-written expected direction\n\nBaseline for all speedups is **A0**. Every arm uses the **same harness, same attention backend, same sampling, same clocks**.\n\n| Arm | Config | Hypothesis tested | Expected direction — prefill (compute-bound) | Expected direction — decode B=1 (BW-bound) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A0** | BF16/FP16, FP16 KV | Reference; establishes harness overhead ≈ 0 | 1.00 by definition | 1.00 |\n| **A1** | W4A16 weight-only (AWQ-style), FP16 KV | How much of the win is *free* from shrinking weights alone, without activation quant | ≈ **0.9–1.0×** (bandwidth win is off the table; GEMM dequant overhead may hurt) | **≈ 3.5–3.8×** ceiling (weight bytes dominate: analytic KV share at B=1 is 0.4–0.5 % at 7B/13B) |\n| **A2** | QuaRot W4A4, **KV16** | Does activation quant + rotation help/hurt once weights are already 4-bit? | ≥ A1; tests whether INT4 GEMM actually uses the ISA | vs A1 ≈ neutral-to-worse (extra quant/rotate traffic, no byte saving) |\n| **A3** | **QuaRot W4A4 + 4-bit KV (full paper config)** | The headline end-to-end claim | ≈ A2 (KV irrelevant at prefill-time compute) | **≥ A2**, largest where KV share is big; at 7B B=64 S=4096 KV is **91 %** of traffic (analytic) vs only **0.9 %** at 70B → arm is strongly model/shape-dependent |\n| **A4** | A3 **minus online Hadamard on `down_proj`** | Isolates the online-Hadamard cost (and its quality necessity) | Cost should be small in FLOPs (analytic: **0.04–0.07 % of layer FLOP**) → if A4 ≫ A3, the cost is *plumbing*, not arithmetic | Same; expect decode-visible gap only via extra unfused kernels/traffic |\n| **A5** | A3 with **KV dequant but no KV quant** (KV16 = A2) / and KV-rotate off | Isolates KV-quantization from activation-quantization | ~0 effect expected | Positive effect ∝ KV share ⇒ **large at 7B/13B, near-inert at 70B (GQA)** — a built-in falsifiable contrast |\n| **A6** | **Offline-rotations only** (no online transforms, naive quantization) | Shows why online Hadamards exist | ≈ A3 in speed | ≈ A3 in speed, but **quality collapse expected** → the quality half of A3's value |\n| **A7** | W8A8 INT (optional ceiling) | Where does the quality/speed frontier bend vs a \"safe\" 8-bit scheme | between A0 and A3 | between A0 and A1 |\n| **A3-H** | A3 on H100 `[A_alt]` | ISA-pinning control: prefill gain must shrink (~2× ceiling) while **decode B=1 speedup should be nearly unchanged** | predicted ↓ vs A100 | predicted ≈ unchanged |\n\n**Key design property:** A1→A2→A3 is an additive ladder (weights → activations → KV), so the decode speedup decomposes into weight-byte, activation-overhead and KV-byte contributions; A4/A6 vs A3 attributes the rotation machinery; A3 vs A3-H attributes the ISA.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Non-arm controls (first-class, not footnotes)\n\n- **C1 — Matched-shape GEMM microbenchmark (kernel-efficiency control).** For every linear projection shape (h×h, h×ffn, ffn×h) in each model, run FP16 vs INT4 GEMM at prefill-M (B·S ∈ {1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}) and decode-M (B ∈ {1,8,32,64}), with and without the fused dequant/quant epilogue. This yields an **achievable-kernel-efficiency ratio** that is the yardstick for judging end-to-end prefill. It separates *\"quantization is bad\"* from *\"this INT4 GEMM kernel is immature.\"*\n- **C2 — Rotation plumbing toggle.** fused vs unfused application of every online Hadamard; also block-size sweep. Report **online-Hadamard time as % of step time**, never averaged away.\n- **C3 — Hadamard arithmetic-only microbench** (square matrices, sizes matching h and ffn) to price the transform independently of the model.\n- **C4 — Long-context ladder, reported as marginal cost.** S ∈ {128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, 16384, 32768}: report **d(latency)/dS for A3 minus A0** as its own curve; **do not fold into a mean**. Analytic prediction to test: a *periodic full-cache* Hadamard refresh costs S·h·log₂h per layer, which equals one 7B decode step around **S ≈ 4k** and ≈ 8× at S = 32k → the crossover is expected inside the ladder, and its location is the finding. (Refresh policy itself is **[ASSUMPTION]**; parameterized as \"every N tokens\".)\n- **C5 — Identical-execution-state controls.** CUDA-graph on/off and `torch.compile` state **identical across all arms** (primary: CUDA-graphs on; secondary repeat with graphs off — the *difference* is reported as launch-overhead sensitivity). Analytic launch sensitivity computed here: 2–4 extra kernels/layer × ~5 µs = **0.3–1.6 ms/token** for 7B–70B [ASSUMPTION: launch cost], comparable to an entire batch-1 decode step → graphs-off is an informative condition, not noise.\n- **C6 — Clock/environment lock.** `nvidia-smi -lgc` locked clocks, ECC on, MPS off, no co-tenants, warmup ≥ 10 iters, ≥ 30 timed iters (prefill) / ≥ 200 generated tokens × ≥ 5 repeats (decode), **median + IQR**, 3 independent process restarts per cell, randomized run order (anti-thermal-drift), same GPU PCI-BDF.\n- **C7 — Attention-path control.** A5/KV16 vs A3/4-bit KV changes the attention kernel's byte load *and* possibly its implementation. Require the **same fused attention kernel** in both, with an explicit dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant variant reported separately.\n- **C8 — Seeds.** ≥ 3 **rotation seeds** × 1 calibration seed for the full grid; plus ≥ 3 **calibration seeds at 7B only** to price calibration variance. Report checkpoint-to-checkpoint spread as an error bar alongside the timing CI, since a rotation-seed-dependent PPL would otherwise masquerade as a scheme effect.\n- **C9 — Harness-overhead control.** A0 run through the QuaRot execution path (rotation matrices set to identity, quantizers set to pass-through). Any residual slowdown is harness tax and is subtracted from every arm's interpretation, reported explicitly rather than silently.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Config matrix\n\n| Axis | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Models | 7B, 13B (primary); 70B (secondary, §8 rule) |\n| Arms | A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6 (+A7 optional) × {7B,13B}; A3/A0 only for A3-H on H100 |\n| Prefill S | 128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096 |\n| Prefill B | 1, 8, 32, 64 (headline cell: S≥1024 ∧ B≥8) |\n| Decode prompt / gen | prompt ∈ {128, 4096}, gen = 128 (4096-prompt: gen 256) |\n| Decode B | 1 (headline), 8, 32, 64 |\n| Quant group size | 128 **[ASSUMPTION — repo default unverifiable here]**, sensitivity at 64/128 for A3 at 7B |\n| Long-context ladder | 128 → 32768, A3 vs A0 only |\n| Quality | WikiText-2 (len-512, non-overlapping), C4 subset, plus zero-shot diagnostic set |\n| Rotation seed | 3 | Calibration seed | 3 (7B only) |\n| Reps | 3 process restarts per timing cell |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Measurement protocol\n\n**Operational definitions (pre-registered):**\n- **TTFT** = t(sampled first token) − t(prefill dispatch), *including* `lm_head` + sampling, *excluding* tokenization/H2D. Reported per (S, B).\n- **Prefill throughput** = (B·S)/(prefill latency), tokens/s.\n- **Decode per-token latency** = (step time) at steady state after prefill, median over generated tokens (first 5 discarded).\n- **Memory footprint** = `max_memory_allocated` + reserved, split into **weights / KV cache / activations**, measured per arm; plus the *analytic* bytes-moved/token as a covariate so measured-vs-predicted can be compared.\n- **Arithmetic intensity** per cell = analytic FLOPs ÷ analytic bytes moved; used as the x-axis of the regime map.\n\n**Procedure per cell:** load arm → warmup → N timed iterations → per-op breakdown via profiler (`torch.profiler` + one Nsight Systems trace per representative cell, not per cell) → dump per-run CSV with git commit, GPU UUID, clock readings, all seeds. Nothing is aggregated across GPUs or across CUDA-graph states.\n\n**Quality protocol:** FP16 reference PPL from the *same* eval script and same tokenization; ΔPPL = PPL(arm) − PPL(A0), computed **per checkpoint** then summarized. Zero-shot battery reported as Δ-accuracy, diagnostic only, never gating.\n\n**Pre-registered quality gates (\"quality-neutral\" operational definition):**\n- **PASS** if ΔPPL(WikiText-2) ≤ **+0.10** and ΔPPL(C4) ≤ **+0.15** (mean over rotation seeds), and no seed exceeds +0.25.\n- **MARGINAL** if ≤ +0.25 → speedups reported but explicitly labelled \"quality-degraded\".\n- **FAIL** if > +0.25 → arm's speedups are reported as *unusable-in-practice*, not as wins.\n- A speedup is quotable as a **win** only for arms passing PASS.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n\n**(a) Regime map (headline figure).** x-axis = arithmetic intensity (analytic, log scale), y-axis = speedup vs A0, points = config cells, colour = arm, panels = {7B, 13B, 70B} × {A100, H100}. Expected structure: a plateau ≈ byte-compression ratio at low intensity (decode), decaying toward the ISA ceiling at high intensity (prefill), with a knee at M\\*. **Findings are reported as the shape of this curve plus the two headline cells, never as an average.**\n\n**(b) Additive attribution on the decode ladder.** Δms/token: A0→A1 (weight bytes), A1→A2 (activation quant + rotation), A2→A3 (KV quant). Each delta carries a CI from seed × restart variance.\n\n**(c) Attribution accounting identity (the core anti-confound rule).** For each cell,\n`T(arm) − T(A0) = Σ ΔT_GEMM + T_rotate + T_quant + T_KV + T_resid`,\nwith `ΔT_GEMM` **taken from C1 microbenchmarks, not from the arm itself**. Then:\n- `|T_resid| ≤ 20 %` of the gap → attribution **accepted**, scheme-level conclusion allowed.\n- `|T_resid| > 20 %` → conclusion is **blocked**; report \"unexplained overhead, attribution unresolved\".\n\n**(d) Explicit decision rules (stated before any measurement).**\n- **\"W4A4 helps decode but not prefill\"** is claimable **iff** all of: (i) decode B=1 speedup CI lower bound > 1.05× for A3 on ≥ 2 of 3 restarts; (ii) the prefill headline cell speedup CI **contains 1.0 or lies below it**; (iii) A3 passes the quality gate; (iv) C1 shows the INT4 GEMM reaching **≥ 0.7× of its ISA-expected efficiency** at prefill shapes — i.e. the prefill failure is not a broken kernel; (v) the same holds on the pinned hardware, stated separately for A100 and H100.\n- **\"Kernel immaturity, not the quantization scheme\"** is the attribution **iff** C1's INT4:FP16 efficiency ratio is **< 0.5×** the datasheet-implied ratio **and** the end-to-end prefill shortfall is accounted for within ±20 % by the C1 deficit plus measured rotate/quant time (condition (iv) fails). In that case the paper-facing claim is *\"the scheme's prefill potential is not realized by the available kernels\"*, and prefill numbers are reported as **lower bounds**.\n- **\"Rotation machinery is affordable\"** iff A3 − A4 speed gap ≤ 5 % **and** A3 quality − A6 quality gap is in the intended direction (A3 clearly better). If A3 − A4 gap > 15 %, the finding is *plumbing-bound*, reported as such.\n- **\"KV quantization is worthwhile\"** iff A3 − A5 is positive with a CI excluding zero at **both** 7B B=64 S=4096 (high KV share) and B=1 (low share). Predicted-as-inert at 70B is itself a reportable outcome, not a failure.\n- **Capacity claim (separate from speed):** W4A4 weights for 70B are **39 GB analytic vs 145.5 GB FP16** → 70B may become *single-GPU-feasible*, which is reported as a footprint result even where speedups are null.\n\n**(e) Statistics.** Median + IQR over cells; bootstrap CI over restarts × seeds; **no pooling across hardware**; Holm correction across the arm-comparison family; every figure traceable to a CSV row.\n\n**(f) External anchor.** Published QuaRot latency/PPL table reproduced into a clearly-labelled *externally reported* appendix table, used only to flag gross inconsistencies (e.g. > 2× off in the same regime ⇒ harness bug hunt), never merged into CIs.\n\n---\n\n## 7. CPU-feasible dry-run — scope and results obtained here\n\n**Scope (permitted):** arithmetic from LLaMA-2 architecture constants **[ASSUMPTION: taken from publicly known configs; no local config/weights exist here]** and vendor datasheet specs **[ASSUMPTION: no GPU present]** — weight/KV footprints, bytes-moved/token, arithmetic-intensity crossovers, overhead-free ceilings, Hadamard marginal-cost ratios, GPU-hour budget. **Nothing here is a latency, throughput or PPL measurement.**\n\nComputed outputs (to ship as the design's predicted-regime table):\n\n```\nFootprint (analytic, group-128 scales):        Regime crossover M* (tokens), A100 [datasheet]:\n 7B  FP16 12.3GB -> W4 3.45GB   KV 512->128 KB/tok   FP16 ~153   W8 ~158   W4 ~81\n 13B FP16 23.9GB -> W4 6.58GB   KV 800->200 KB/tok   => decode (B<=64) bandwidth-bound;\n 70B FP16 145.5GB-> W4 39.0GB   KV 320->80  KB/tok      prefill (S*B>=1024) compute-bound\n\nDecode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 (analytic, weights+KV):\n 7B  B=1 S=128: 3.57x (KV share 0.5%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 91.2%)\n 13B B=1 S=128: 3.64x (KV share 0.4%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 89.3%)\n 70B B=1 S=4096:3.73x (KV share 0.9%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.82x (KV share 35.5%)\n => KV-quant performance effect is large at 7B/13B high-B*S, nearly inert at 70B (GQA)\n\nOverhead-free ceilings: A100 compute-bound 4.0x, byte-bound 3.76x | H100 (no INT4 TC) compute-bound 2.0x\nOnline Hadamard per token/layer: 0.01-0.07% of layer FLOP, 16-112 KB extra RW traffic\n => cost is plumbing/launch (0.3-1.6 ms/token if unfused), NOT arithmetic\nFull-KV Hadamard refresh vs one decode step (7B): S=1k 0.25x | S=4k 0.99x | S=16k 3.98x | S=32k 7.96x\n => long-context ladder must span 128..32768 to capture the crossover\n```\n\n**Explicitly out of scope:** no latency, no tokens/s, no PPL, no accuracy, no \"expected speedup we obtained\".\n\n---\n\n## 8. Resources / runtime estimate — **estimates, marked as such**\n\nAnalytic FLOP-based budget, A100 @ 40 % assumed MFU **[ASSUMPTION: MFU unknown; scales linearly with it]**, 7 arms, 3 seeds, per §4 grid:\n\n| Model | perf sweeps | offline quant | PPL eval | total (GPU·h) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7B | ~3.2 | ~0.05 | ~0.4 | **~3.7** |\n| 13B | ~6.3 | ~0.1 | ~0.8 | **~7.2** |\n| 70B (2-GPU, sharding overhead not included) | ~38.3 | ~0.6 | ~5.1 | **~44+** |\n\nPlus microbenchmarks C1–C3 (~1–2 GPU·h) and one H100 A3/A0 repeat (~2 GPU·h on H100). **Total ≈ 15 GPU·h for the primary 7B+13B study; 70B adds ~3× that**, i.e. 70B is ~75 % of total cost for 2 of ~10 reportable contrasts.\n\n**Pre-registered 70B drop rule:** run 70B only if (i) ≥ 2×80 GB are actually available, (ii) projected 70B cost ≤ 40 % of the approved GPU-hour budget, and (iii) the sharded harness reproduces 7B A0 timings within 5 % of the single-GPU case (harness sanity). If any fails, **drop 70B to quality-only** (A0/A3 ΔPPL + zero-shot, no timing) and say so in the limitations; do not silently shrink the primary grid.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Risks / limitations\n\n1. **Kernel maturity dominates the prefill conclusion.** Mitigated by C1/C9 and the ±20 % residual rule, but never eliminated; prefill numbers may only be lower bounds.\n2. **Hardware ISA is a design axis, not a detail.** On H100 the prefill ceiling roughly halves while the decode ceiling is unchanged — pooling would be a category error.\n3. **Rotation/Hadamard fusion state of the shipped code is unknown [ASSUMPTION]** → C2/C5 treat it as an experimental factor rather than a fixed property.\n4. **70B GQA makes KV ablations inert**; a null A3-vs-A5 there must be pre-declared as *expected*, else it reads as a contradiction of the 7B result.\n5. **KV dequant may force a different attention kernel**, which alone can move decode latency (C7).\n6. **Per-token overhead is launch-bound at batch 1** → CUDA-graph state changes the answer; both states reported.\n7. **Calibration/rotation seed variance** may exceed the arm effect for quality; hence C8 and per-checkpoint ΔPPL.\n8. **PPL-insensitive damage** (long-context, code, reasoning) is out of scope; zero-shot battery is diagnostic only.\n9. **Non-determinism / thermal drift** handled by clocks-lock + randomized order, not by more averaging.\n10. **No verification of the repository is possible in this environment** — every repo-specific setting (group size, rotation construction, calibration set, kernel flags, whether `lm_head`/embeddings stay FP16) is a parameterized assumption, not a fact.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Deliverable artifacts (to be produced when compute exists)\n\nconfig matrix YAML · arm builder (identity-rotation pass-through path for C9) · timing harness with per-op profiler hooks · C1 microbench suite · ΔPPL/eval script · attribution-residual analysis script → regime-map figure + ladder-decomposition table + appendix external-anchor table · per-run CSV archive with environment provenance.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Assumptions vs verified facts (final separation)\n\n**Verified here:** `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (no QuaRot repo, no weights/config, no harness, no kernels, no calibration data); network dead (DNS failure for pypi + github); Python 3.14.4 with **no** torch/transformers/numpy/etc.; no GPU device (`nvidia-smi` cannot reach driver, no `/dev/nvidia*`); 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, gcc-15/git present. The §7 numbers are arithmetic I ran in this sandbox.\n\n**Assumed, not known:** A100-80GB/H100 datasheet bandwidth & TOPS; LLaMA-2 config constants; group size 128; OpenAI-style C4 calibration set (128×2048); offline scale-fitting excluded from timing; refresh policy for KV Hadamards; fusion status of shipped kernels; 40 % MFU for the budget; embeddings/`lm_head` kept in FP16; ≥2 GPUs for 70B.", "evidence": "ENVIRONMENT (executed in sandbox, all successful = not proof of any experiment): `ls -la /workspace` -> only task.json (405 B); `du -sh /workspace` -> 8.0K; `cat /workspace/task.json` -> \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"; `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*quarot*'` -> empty; `~/.cache` absent, no /opt, no *.whl, no libtorch -> no repo, weights, HF configs, harness, CUDA kernels or calibration data obtainable; network dead (urllib to pypi.org and `git ls-remote github.com/spcl/QuaRot` both -> DNS resolution failure); python 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3, pip 25.1.1, find_spec probe shows torch/transformers/numpy/datasets/accelerate/sentencepiece/tokenizers/scipy/pandas/triton/lm_eval all False; `nvidia-smi` -> \"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\", no /dev/nvidia* (CUDA 13.1 toolkit + libcuda.so.595.71.05 stub present but no device); nproc=224, MemTotal ~2.1 TB, Xeon Platinum 8570, gcc-15 + git available, /tmp tmpfs 1 TB. ANALYTIC CPU DRY RUN (pure arithmetic, three scripts run with python3, no GPU/latency/PPL involved): weight footprints 7B 12.3->3.45 GB, 13B 23.9->6.58 GB, 70B 145.5->39.0 GB (group-128 scales); KV bytes/token 7B 512->128 KB, 13B 800->200 KB, 70B 320->80 KB; roofline crossover M* 153 (FP16) vs 81 (W4) tokens on A100 datasheet specs -> justifies bandwidth-bound decode (B<=64) vs compute-bound prefill (S*B>=1024); decode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 3.57-3.96x with KV share 0.4-0.5% at B=1 but 89-91% at B=64,S=4096 for 7B/13B vs only 0.9-35.5% for 70B (GQA) -> predicts A5 (KV ablation) is shape- and model-dependent and near-inert at 70B; overhead-free ceilings 4.0x compute-bound on A100 vs 2.0x on H100 (no INT4 tensor cores), 3.76x weight-byte-bound; online-Hadamard marginal cost 0.011-0.073% of layer FLOP and 16-112 KB/token extra traffic but 0.32-1.6 ms/token if it costs 2-4 unfused kernel launches per layer at ~5 us -> cost is plumbing, not arithmetic; full-KV Hadamard refresh equals one 7B decode step at S~4k and 8x at S=32k -> motivates the 128..32768 ladder; GPU-hour budget ~3.7 (7B), ~7.2 (13B), ~44+ (70B) at an assumed 40% MFU -> 70B is ~75% of cost, which grounds the pre-registered drop rule. NO measured latency, throughput, memory-occupancy or perplexity value was produced or is claimed anywhere.", "assumptions": ["Hardware is A100-80GB with ~2.04 TB/s HBM and ~312 TFLOPS FP16 / INT4 tensor cores, and H100 as the parameterized no-INT4-TC alternative (~3.35 TB/s, ~989 TFLOPS FP16, INT8-pair INT4 path); all such values are vendor datasheet figures, unmeasurable here.", "LLaMA-2 7B/13B/70B architectural constants (hidden size, layers, head counts incl. 70B GQA, FFN width, vocab 32000) are taken from publicly known configs; no config or weight file exists in this sandbox to verify them.", "Quantization uses group size 128 with FP16 scales/zero-points (giving ~0.53 bytes/weight), embeddings and lm_head stay FP16, and calibration uses an OpenAI-style C4 subset of 128x2048 with all scale-fitting performed offline and excluded from timing.", "The QuaRot-style implementation exposes independent toggles for offline rotations, the down_proj online Hadamard, KV-cache Hadamard and KV quantization, and permits an identity-rotation pass-through path for the harness-overhead control.", "The KV-cache Hadamard refresh policy (how often and over what span the cache is re-rotated) is a parameter, not a known constant; the long-context ladder is designed to locate the crossover rather than assume one.", "Attention backend is held constant across arms, with dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant treated as an explicit factor.", "Runtime-efficiency figures in the resource budget assume 40% MFU; the estimate scales linearly with the true value.", "At least 2x80GB GPUs are available only for the optional 70B arm; the primary study is single-GPU.", "Quality-neutral is operationally defined by the pre-registered band: mean delta WikiText-2 PPL <= 0.10, delta C4 <= 0.15, no seed above 0.25."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the available INT4 GEMM kernel realizes anywhere near its ISA-expected throughput at prefill shapes; if it does not, prefill results are lower bounds and must be attributed to kernel maturity rather than to the quantization scheme.", "The sequence-length crossover at which online/periodic Hadamard cost cancels the 4-bit decode gain; the analytic estimate places it near S~4k for 7B but the true location depends on the implementation's refresh policy and fusion.", "Whether the activation-quantization step (A1 -> A2 on the decode ladder) is net-neutral or net-negative at batch 1; the byte analysis says no saving, the overhead is unquantified.", "Whether 4-bit KV degrades quality differently across model sizes, and whether the KV speed benefit at 70B is genuinely inert due to GQA.", "Actual shipped-kernel fusion state, rotation construction details, group size and calibration settings of the QuaRot code - unverifiable without the repository, hence parameterized.", "True MFU for both FP16 and INT4 paths, and the real wall-clock cost of 70B tensor-parallel sharding, which is excluded from the budget estimate.", "Whether the published QuaRot latency table is comparable to this harness at all (different serving stack, clocks and kernels); it is used only as a gross-consistency anchor.", "No measurement of any kind is possible in this sandbox, so every quantitative statement in the design remains analytic or placeholder until executed elsewhere."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b3b8318ca9810ab0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b3b8318ca9810ab0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# QuaRot W4A4 on LLaMA-2 \\u2014 Experiment Design (prefill vs decode)\\n\\n**Status:** design-only. Nothing here is a measured result. All quantitative values below are either **analytic arithmetic** (computed in this sandbox, formulas shown) or **placeholders**. Facts that could not be verified in this environment are tagged **[ASSUMPTION]**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Scope fixed by researcher approval\\n\\n| Item | Fixed decision |\\n|---|---|\\n| Hardware | **A100-80GB**, 1 GPU for 7B/13B. **H100 parameterized as the \\\"no-INT4-tensor-core\\\" alternative condition** `[A_alt]`; prefill conclusions are hardware-pinned and never pooled across the two. |\\n| Models | LLaMA-2 **7B + 13B primary**; **70B optional**, needs \\u22652\\u00d780 GB, with a pre-registered drop rule (\\u00a78). |\\n| Quality | **Perplexity primary** (WikiText-2 len-512, C4 subset); zero-shot battery = secondary/diagnostic. Pre-registered \\u0394PPL band defines \\\"quality-neutral\\\". |\\n| Headline | **Two regime-specific speedups**: decode = batch-1 per-token latency; prefill = large-batch (S\\u22651024, B\\u22658) tokens/s + TTFT. **No single \\\"average speedup\\\" is ever reported.** |\\n| Published QuaRot numbers | Sanity **reference anchor only**, separate table, explicitly labelled *externally reported*, never inside our CIs. |\\n| CPU dry run | Included; **arithmetic only** (FLOP/byte accounting, roofline regime map, bytes-moved/token, KV-bytes/token). No latency/PPL claim from it. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Rationale \\u2014 the causal model the design tests\\n\\nTwo different bottlenecks are in play, so a single scalar answer is meaningless:\\n\\n- **Decode at B=1** is **memory-bandwidth-bound**. The only thing that helps is moving fewer bytes: weights (2 B \\u2192 ~0.53 B with group-128 scales) and KV cache (2 B \\u2192 0.5 B). Ceiling \\u2248 weight/KV byte-compression ratio, **independent of INT4 tensor cores**. QuaRot adds *new* per-token work (activation quantization, online Hadamards) that is FLOP-cheap but **traffic- and launch-heavy** \\u2192 can eat the gain.\\n- **Prefill at S\\u00b7B \\u2265 1024** is **compute-bound**. Gain ceiling = (INT4-path peak TOPS)/(FP16 peak), i.e. **hardware-pinned**: ~4\\u00d7 on A100 (INT4 TC) but only ~2\\u00d7 on H100 (no INT4 TC \\u2192 INT8-pair path) **[ASSUMPTION: datasheet specs]**. Plus per-layer quantize/rotate overhead that FP16 does not pay.\\n\\nSo the design's central object is **speedup as a function of arithmetic intensity**, and the causal question is *which component (weight bytes, KV bytes, GEMM ISA, rotation, quantization) supplies the gain or the cost in each regime*.\\n\\n**Analytic regime justification (computed here, A100 datasheet values [ASSUMPTION]):** memory\\u2192compute crossover token-count M\\\\* = (peak/bandwidth)\\u00b7bytes_per_elem/2 \\u2192 **FP16 M\\\\* \\u2248 153 tokens, W4 M\\\\* \\u2248 81 tokens**. Decode at B \\u2264 64 sits far below \\u2192 bandwidth-bound \\u2714. Prefill at S\\u00b7B \\u2265 1024 sits far above \\u2192 compute-bound \\u2714. These definitions are therefore not arbitrary.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Arms, each tied to a hypothesis with a pre-written expected direction\\n\\nBaseline for all speedups is **A0**. Every arm uses the **same harness, same attention backend, same sampling, same clocks**.\\n\\n| Arm | Config | Hypothesis tested | Expected direction \\u2014 prefill (compute-bound) | Expected direction \\u2014 decode B=1 (BW-bound) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **A0** | BF16/FP16, FP16 KV | Reference; establishes harness overhead \\u2248 0 | 1.00 by definition | 1.00 |\\n| **A1** | W4A16 weight-only (AWQ-style), FP16 KV | How much of the win is *free* from shrinking weights alone, without activation quant | \\u2248 **0.9\\u20131.0\\u00d7** (bandwidth win is off the table; GEMM dequant overhead may hurt) | **\\u2248 3.5\\u20133.8\\u00d7** ceiling (weight bytes dominate: analytic KV share at B=1 is 0.4\\u20130.5 % at 7B/13B) |\\n| **A2** | QuaRot W4A4, **KV16** | Does activation quant + rotation help/hurt once weights are already 4-bit? | \\u2265 A1; tests whether INT4 GEMM actually uses the ISA | vs A1 \\u2248 neutral-to-worse (extra quant/rotate traffic, no byte saving) |\\n| **A3** | **QuaRot W4A4 + 4-bit KV (full paper config)** | The headline end-to-end claim | \\u2248 A2 (KV irrelevant at prefill-time compute) | **\\u2265 A2**, largest where KV share is big; at 7B B=64 S=4096 KV is **91 %** of traffic (analytic) vs only **0.9 %** at 70B \\u2192 arm is strongly model/shape-dependent |\\n| **A4** | A3 **minus online Hadamard on `down_proj`** | Isolates the online-Hadamard cost (and its quality necessity) | Cost should be small in FLOPs (analytic: **0.04\\u20130.07 % of layer FLOP**) \\u2192 if A4 \\u226b A3, the cost is *plumbing*, not arithmetic | Same; expect decode-visible gap only via extra unfused kernels/traffic |\\n| **A5** | A3 with **KV dequant but no KV quant** (KV16 = A2) / and KV-rotate off | Isolates KV-quantization from activation-quantization | ~0 effect expected | Positive effect \\u221d KV share \\u21d2 **large at 7B/13B, near-inert at 70B (GQA)** \\u2014 a built-in falsifiable contrast |\\n| **A6** | **Offline-rotations only** (no online transforms, naive quantization) | Shows why online Hadamards exist | \\u2248 A3 in speed | \\u2248 A3 in speed, but **quality collapse expected** \\u2192 the quality half of A3's value |\\n| **A7** | W8A8 INT (optional ceiling) | Where does the quality/speed frontier bend vs a \\\"safe\\\" 8-bit scheme | between A0 and A3 | between A0 and A1 |\\n| **A3-H** | A3 on H100 `[A_alt]` | ISA-pinning control: prefill gain must shrink (~2\\u00d7 ceiling) while **decode B=1 speedup should be nearly unchanged** | predicted \\u2193 vs A100 | predicted \\u2248 unchanged |\\n\\n**Key design property:** A1\\u2192A2\\u2192A3 is an additive ladder (weights \\u2192 activations \\u2192 KV), so the decode speedup decomposes into weight-byte, activation-overhead and KV-byte contributions; A4/A6 vs A3 attributes the rotation machinery; A3 vs A3-H attributes the ISA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Non-arm controls (first-class, not footnotes)\\n\\n- **C1 \\u2014 Matched-shape GEMM microbenchmark (kernel-efficiency control).** For every linear projection shape (h\\u00d7h, h\\u00d7ffn, ffn\\u00d7h) in each model, run FP16 vs INT4 GEMM at prefill-M (B\\u00b7S \\u2208 {1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}) and decode-M (B \\u2208 {1,8,32,64}), with and without the fused dequant/quant epilogue. This yields an **achievable-kernel-efficiency ratio** that is the yardstick for judging end-to-end prefill. It separates *\\\"quantization is bad\\\"* from *\\\"this INT4 GEMM kernel is immature.\\\"*\\n- **C2 \\u2014 Rotation plumbing toggle.** fused vs unfused application of every online Hadamard; also block-size sweep. Report **online-Hadamard time as % of step time**, never averaged away.\\n- **C3 \\u2014 Hadamard arithmetic-only microbench** (square matrices, sizes matching h and ffn) to price the transform independently of the model.\\n- **C4 \\u2014 Long-context ladder, reported as marginal cost.** S \\u2208 {128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, 16384, 32768}: report **d(latency)/dS for A3 minus A0** as its own curve; **do not fold into a mean**. Analytic prediction to test: a *periodic full-cache* Hadamard refresh costs S\\u00b7h\\u00b7log\\u2082h per layer, which equals one 7B decode step around **S \\u2248 4k** and \\u2248 8\\u00d7 at S = 32k \\u2192 the crossover is expected inside the ladder, and its location is the finding. (Refresh policy itself is **[ASSUMPTION]**; parameterized as \\\"every N tokens\\\".)\\n- **C5 \\u2014 Identical-execution-state controls.** CUDA-graph on/off and `torch.compile` state **identical across all arms** (primary: CUDA-graphs on; secondary repeat with graphs off \\u2014 the *difference* is reported as launch-overhead sensitivity). Analytic launch sensitivity computed here: 2\\u20134 extra kernels/layer \\u00d7 ~5 \\u00b5s = **0.3\\u20131.6 ms/token** for 7B\\u201370B [ASSUMPTION: launch cost], comparable to an entire batch-1 decode step \\u2192 graphs-off is an informative condition, not noise.\\n- **C6 \\u2014 Clock/environment lock.** `nvidia-smi -lgc` locked clocks, ECC on, MPS off, no co-tenants, warmup \\u2265 10 iters, \\u2265 30 timed iters (prefill) / \\u2265 200 generated tokens \\u00d7 \\u2265 5 repeats (decode), **median + IQR**, 3 independent process restarts per cell, randomized run order (anti-thermal-drift), same GPU PCI-BDF.\\n- **C7 \\u2014 Attention-path control.** A5/KV16 vs A3/4-bit KV changes the attention kernel's byte load *and* possibly its implementation. Require the **same fused attention kernel** in both, with an explicit dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant variant reported separately.\\n- **C8 \\u2014 Seeds.** \\u2265 3 **rotation seeds** \\u00d7 1 calibration seed for the full grid; plus \\u2265 3 **calibration seeds at 7B only** to price calibration variance. Report checkpoint-to-checkpoint spread as an error bar alongside the timing CI, since a rotation-seed-dependent PPL would otherwise masquerade as a scheme effect.\\n- **C9 \\u2014 Harness-overhead control.** A0 run through the QuaRot execution path (rotation matrices set to identity, quantizers set to pass-through). Any residual slowdown is harness tax and is subtracted from every arm's interpretation, reported explicitly rather than silently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Config matrix\\n\\n| Axis | Values |\\n|---|---|\\n| Models | 7B, 13B (primary); 70B (secondary, \\u00a78 rule) |\\n| Arms | A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6 (+A7 optional) \\u00d7 {7B,13B}; A3/A0 only for A3-H on H100 |\\n| Prefill S | 128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096 |\\n| Prefill B | 1, 8, 32, 64 (headline cell: S\\u22651024 \\u2227 B\\u22658) |\\n| Decode prompt / gen | prompt \\u2208 {128, 4096}, gen = 128 (4096-prompt: gen 256) |\\n| Decode B | 1 (headline), 8, 32, 64 |\\n| Quant group size | 128 **[ASSUMPTION \\u2014 repo default unverifiable here]**, sensitivity at 64/128 for A3 at 7B |\\n| Long-context ladder | 128 \\u2192 32768, A3 vs A0 only |\\n| Quality | WikiText-2 (len-512, non-overlapping), C4 subset, plus zero-shot diagnostic set |\\n| Rotation seed | 3 | Calibration seed | 3 (7B only) |\\n| Reps | 3 process restarts per timing cell |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Measurement protocol\\n\\n**Operational definitions (pre-registered):**\\n- **TTFT** = t(sampled first token) \\u2212 t(prefill dispatch), *including* `lm_head` + sampling, *excluding* tokenization/H2D. Reported per (S, B).\\n- **Prefill throughput** = (B\\u00b7S)/(prefill latency), tokens/s.\\n- **Decode per-token latency** = (step time) at steady state after prefill, median over generated tokens (first 5 discarded).\\n- **Memory footprint** = `max_memory_allocated` + reserved, split into **weights / KV cache / activations**, measured per arm; plus the *analytic* bytes-moved/token as a covariate so measured-vs-predicted can be compared.\\n- **Arithmetic intensity** per cell = analytic FLOPs \\u00f7 analytic bytes moved; used as the x-axis of the regime map.\\n\\n**Procedure per cell:** load arm \\u2192 warmup \\u2192 N timed iterations \\u2192 per-op breakdown via profiler (`torch.profiler` + one Nsight Systems trace per representative cell, not per cell) \\u2192 dump per-run CSV with git commit, GPU UUID, clock readings, all seeds. Nothing is aggregated across GPUs or across CUDA-graph states.\\n\\n**Quality protocol:** FP16 reference PPL from the *same* eval script and same tokenization; \\u0394PPL = PPL(arm) \\u2212 PPL(A0), computed **per checkpoint** then summarized. Zero-shot battery reported as \\u0394-accuracy, diagnostic only, never gating.\\n\\n**Pre-registered quality gates (\\\"quality-neutral\\\" operational definition):**\\n- **PASS** if \\u0394PPL(WikiText-2) \\u2264 **+0.10** and \\u0394PPL(C4) \\u2264 **+0.15** (mean over rotation seeds), and no seed exceeds +0.25.\\n- **MARGINAL** if \\u2264 +0.25 \\u2192 speedups reported but explicitly labelled \\\"quality-degraded\\\".\\n- **FAIL** if > +0.25 \\u2192 arm's speedups are reported as *unusable-in-practice*, not as wins.\\n- A speedup is quotable as a **win** only for arms passing PASS.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan\\n\\n**(a) Regime map (headline figure).** x-axis = arithmetic intensity (analytic, log scale), y-axis = speedup vs A0, points = config cells, colour = arm, panels = {7B, 13B, 70B} \\u00d7 {A100, H100}. Expected structure: a plateau \\u2248 byte-compression ratio at low intensity (decode), decaying toward the ISA ceiling at high intensity (prefill), with a knee at M\\\\*. **Findings are reported as the shape of this curve plus the two headline cells, never as an average.**\\n\\n**(b) Additive attribution on the decode ladder.** \\u0394ms/token: A0\\u2192A1 (weight bytes), A1\\u2192A2 (activation quant + rotation), A2\\u2192A3 (KV quant). Each delta carries a CI from seed \\u00d7 restart variance.\\n\\n**(c) Attribution accounting identity (the core anti-confound rule).** For each cell,\\n`T(arm) \\u2212 T(A0) = \\u03a3 \\u0394T_GEMM + T_rotate + T_quant + T_KV + T_resid`,\\nwith `\\u0394T_GEMM` **taken from C1 microbenchmarks, not from the arm itself**. Then:\\n- `|T_resid| \\u2264 20 %` of the gap \\u2192 attribution **accepted**, scheme-level conclusion allowed.\\n- `|T_resid| > 20 %` \\u2192 conclusion is **blocked**; report \\\"unexplained overhead, attribution unresolved\\\".\\n\\n**(d) Explicit decision rules (stated before any measurement).**\\n- **\\\"W4A4 helps decode but not prefill\\\"** is claimable **iff** all of: (i) decode B=1 speedup CI lower bound > 1.05\\u00d7 for A3 on \\u2265 2 of 3 restarts; (ii) the prefill headline cell speedup CI **contains 1.0 or lies below it**; (iii) A3 passes the quality gate; (iv) C1 shows the INT4 GEMM reaching **\\u2265 0.7\\u00d7 of its ISA-expected efficiency** at prefill shapes \\u2014 i.e. the prefill failure is not a broken kernel; (v) the same holds on the pinned hardware, stated separately for A100 and H100.\\n- **\\\"Kernel immaturity, not the quantization scheme\\\"** is the attribution **iff** C1's INT4:FP16 efficiency ratio is **< 0.5\\u00d7** the datasheet-implied ratio **and** the end-to-end prefill shortfall is accounted for within \\u00b120 % by the C1 deficit plus measured rotate/quant time (condition (iv) fails). In that case the paper-facing claim is *\\\"the scheme's prefill potential is not realized by the available kernels\\\"*, and prefill numbers are reported as **lower bounds**.\\n- **\\\"Rotation machinery is affordable\\\"** iff A3 \\u2212 A4 speed gap \\u2264 5 % **and** A3 quality \\u2212 A6 quality gap is in the intended direction (A3 clearly better). If A3 \\u2212 A4 gap > 15 %, the finding is *plumbing-bound*, reported as such.\\n- **\\\"KV quantization is worthwhile\\\"** iff A3 \\u2212 A5 is positive with a CI excluding zero at **both** 7B B=64 S=4096 (high KV share) and B=1 (low share). Predicted-as-inert at 70B is itself a reportable outcome, not a failure.\\n- **Capacity claim (separate from speed):** W4A4 weights for 70B are **39 GB analytic vs 145.5 GB FP16** \\u2192 70B may become *single-GPU-feasible*, which is reported as a footprint result even where speedups are null.\\n\\n**(e) Statistics.** Median + IQR over cells; bootstrap CI over restarts \\u00d7 seeds; **no pooling across hardware**; Holm correction across the arm-comparison family; every figure traceable to a CSV row.\\n\\n**(f) External anchor.** Published QuaRot latency/PPL table reproduced into a clearly-labelled *externally reported* appendix table, used only to flag gross inconsistencies (e.g. > 2\\u00d7 off in the same regime \\u21d2 harness bug hunt), never merged into CIs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. CPU-feasible dry-run \\u2014 scope and results obtained here\\n\\n**Scope (permitted):** arithmetic from LLaMA-2 architecture constants **[ASSUMPTION: taken from publicly known configs; no local config/weights exist here]** and vendor datasheet specs **[ASSUMPTION: no GPU present]** \\u2014 weight/KV footprints, bytes-moved/token, arithmetic-intensity crossovers, overhead-free ceilings, Hadamard marginal-cost ratios, GPU-hour budget. **Nothing here is a latency, throughput or PPL measurement.**\\n\\nComputed outputs (to ship as the design's predicted-regime table):\\n\\n```\\nFootprint (analytic, group-128 scales):        Regime crossover M* (tokens), A100 [datasheet]:\\n 7B  FP16 12.3GB -> W4 3.45GB   KV 512->128 KB/tok   FP16 ~153   W8 ~158   W4 ~81\\n 13B FP16 23.9GB -> W4 6.58GB   KV 800->200 KB/tok   => decode (B<=64) bandwidth-bound;\\n 70B FP16 145.5GB-> W4 39.0GB   KV 320->80  KB/tok      prefill (S*B>=1024) compute-bound\\n\\nDecode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 (analytic, weights+KV):\\n 7B  B=1 S=128: 3.57x (KV share 0.5%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 91.2%)\\n 13B B=1 S=128: 3.64x (KV share 0.4%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 89.3%)\\n 70B B=1 S=4096:3.73x (KV share 0.9%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.82x (KV share 35.5%)\\n => KV-quant performance effect is large at 7B/13B high-B*S, nearly inert at 70B (GQA)\\n\\nOverhead-free ceilings: A100 compute-bound 4.0x, byte-bound 3.76x | H100 (no INT4 TC) compute-bound 2.0x\\nOnline Hadamard per token/layer: 0.01-0.07% of layer FLOP, 16-112 KB extra RW traffic\\n => cost is plumbing/launch (0.3-1.6 ms/token if unfused), NOT arithmetic\\nFull-KV Hadamard refresh vs one decode step (7B): S=1k 0.25x | S=4k 0.99x | S=16k 3.98x | S=32k 7.96x\\n => long-context ladder must span 128..32768 to capture the crossover\\n```\\n\\n**Explicitly out of scope:** no latency, no tokens/s, no PPL, no accuracy, no \\\"expected speedup we obtained\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Resources / runtime estimate \\u2014 **estimates, marked as such**\\n\\nAnalytic FLOP-based budget, A100 @ 40 % assumed MFU **[ASSUMPTION: MFU unknown; scales linearly with it]**, 7 arms, 3 seeds, per \\u00a74 grid:\\n\\n| Model | perf sweeps | offline quant | PPL eval | total (GPU\\u00b7h) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 7B | ~3.2 | ~0.05 | ~0.4 | **~3.7** |\\n| 13B | ~6.3 | ~0.1 | ~0.8 | **~7.2** |\\n| 70B (2-GPU, sharding overhead not included) | ~38.3 | ~0.6 | ~5.1 | **~44+** |\\n\\nPlus microbenchmarks C1\\u2013C3 (~1\\u20132 GPU\\u00b7h) and one H100 A3/A0 repeat (~2 GPU\\u00b7h on H100). **Total \\u2248 15 GPU\\u00b7h for the primary 7B+13B study; 70B adds ~3\\u00d7 that**, i.e. 70B is ~75 % of total cost for 2 of ~10 reportable contrasts.\\n\\n**Pre-registered 70B drop rule:** run 70B only if (i) \\u2265 2\\u00d780 GB are actually available, (ii) projected 70B cost \\u2264 40 % of the approved GPU-hour budget, and (iii) the sharded harness reproduces 7B A0 timings within 5 % of the single-GPU case (harness sanity). If any fails, **drop 70B to quality-only** (A0/A3 \\u0394PPL + zero-shot, no timing) and say so in the limitations; do not silently shrink the primary grid.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Risks / limitations\\n\\n1. **Kernel maturity dominates the prefill conclusion.** Mitigated by C1/C9 and the \\u00b120 % residual rule, but never eliminated; prefill numbers may only be lower bounds.\\n2. **Hardware ISA is a design axis, not a detail.** On H100 the prefill ceiling roughly halves while the decode ceiling is unchanged \\u2014 pooling would be a category error.\\n3. **Rotation/Hadamard fusion state of the shipped code is unknown [ASSUMPTION]** \\u2192 C2/C5 treat it as an experimental factor rather than a fixed property.\\n4. **70B GQA makes KV ablations inert**; a null A3-vs-A5 there must be pre-declared as *expected*, else it reads as a contradiction of the 7B result.\\n5. **KV dequant may force a different attention kernel**, which alone can move decode latency (C7).\\n6. **Per-token overhead is launch-bound at batch 1** \\u2192 CUDA-graph state changes the answer; both states reported.\\n7. **Calibration/rotation seed variance** may exceed the arm effect for quality; hence C8 and per-checkpoint \\u0394PPL.\\n8. **PPL-insensitive damage** (long-context, code, reasoning) is out of scope; zero-shot battery is diagnostic only.\\n9. **Non-determinism / thermal drift** handled by clocks-lock + randomized order, not by more averaging.\\n10. **No verification of the repository is possible in this environment** \\u2014 every repo-specific setting (group size, rotation construction, calibration set, kernel flags, whether `lm_head`/embeddings stay FP16) is a parameterized assumption, not a fact.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Deliverable artifacts (to be produced when compute exists)\\n\\nconfig matrix YAML \\u00b7 arm builder (identity-rotation pass-through path for C9) \\u00b7 timing harness with per-op profiler hooks \\u00b7 C1 microbench suite \\u00b7 \\u0394PPL/eval script \\u00b7 attribution-residual analysis script \\u2192 regime-map figure + ladder-decomposition table + appendix external-anchor table \\u00b7 per-run CSV archive with environment provenance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Assumptions vs verified facts (final separation)\\n\\n**Verified here:** `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (no QuaRot repo, no weights/config, no harness, no kernels, no calibration data); network dead (DNS failure for pypi + github); Python 3.14.4 with **no** torch/transformers/numpy/etc.; no GPU device (`nvidia-smi` cannot reach driver, no `/dev/nvidia*`); 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, gcc-15/git present. The \\u00a77 numbers are arithmetic I ran in this sandbox.\\n\\n**Assumed, not known:** A100-80GB/H100 datasheet bandwidth & TOPS; LLaMA-2 config constants; group size 128; OpenAI-style C4 calibration set (128\\u00d72048); offline scale-fitting excluded from timing; refresh policy for KV Hadamards; fusion status of shipped kernels; 40 % MFU for the budget; embeddings/`lm_head` kept in FP16; \\u22652 GPUs for 70B.\", \"evidence\": \"ENVIRONMENT (executed in sandbox, all successful = not proof of any experiment): `ls -la /workspace` -> only task.json (405 B); `du -sh /workspace` -> 8.0K; `cat /workspace/task.json` -> \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"; `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*quarot*'` -> empty; `~/.cache` absent, no /opt, no *.whl, no libtorch -> no repo, weights, HF configs, harness, CUDA kernels or calibration data obtainable; network dead (urllib to pypi.org and `git ls-remote github.com/spcl/QuaRot` both -> DNS resolution failure); python 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3, pip 25.1.1, find_spec probe shows torch/transformers/numpy/datasets/accelerate/sentencepiece/tokenizers/scipy/pandas/triton/lm_eval all False; `nvidia-smi` -> \\\"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\", no /dev/nvidia* (CUDA 13.1 toolkit + libcuda.so.595.71.05 stub present but no device); nproc=224, MemTotal ~2.1 TB, Xeon Platinum 8570, gcc-15 + git available, /tmp tmpfs 1 TB. ANALYTIC CPU DRY RUN (pure arithmetic, three scripts run with python3, no GPU/latency/PPL involved): weight footprints 7B 12.3->3.45 GB, 13B 23.9->6.58 GB, 70B 145.5->39.0 GB (group-128 scales); KV bytes/token 7B 512->128 KB, 13B 800->200 KB, 70B 320->80 KB; roofline crossover M* 153 (FP16) vs 81 (W4) tokens on A100 datasheet specs -> justifies bandwidth-bound decode (B<=64) vs compute-bound prefill (S*B>=1024); decode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 3.57-3.96x with KV share 0.4-0.5% at B=1 but 89-91% at B=64,S=4096 for 7B/13B vs only 0.9-35.5% for 70B (GQA) -> predicts A5 (KV ablation) is shape- and model-dependent and near-inert at 70B; overhead-free ceilings 4.0x compute-bound on A100 vs 2.0x on H100 (no INT4 tensor cores), 3.76x weight-byte-bound; online-Hadamard marginal cost 0.011-0.073% of layer FLOP and 16-112 KB/token extra traffic but 0.32-1.6 ms/token if it costs 2-4 unfused kernel launches per layer at ~5 us -> cost is plumbing, not arithmetic; full-KV Hadamard refresh equals one 7B decode step at S~4k and 8x at S=32k -> motivates the 128..32768 ladder; GPU-hour budget ~3.7 (7B), ~7.2 (13B), ~44+ (70B) at an assumed 40% MFU -> 70B is ~75% of cost, which grounds the pre-registered drop rule. NO measured latency, throughput, memory-occupancy or perplexity value was produced or is claimed anywhere.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Hardware is A100-80GB with ~2.04 TB/s HBM and ~312 TFLOPS FP16 / INT4 tensor cores, and H100 as the parameterized no-INT4-TC alternative (~3.35 TB/s, ~989 TFLOPS FP16, INT8-pair INT4 path); all such values are vendor datasheet figures, unmeasurable here.\", \"LLaMA-2 7B/13B/70B architectural constants (hidden size, layers, head counts incl. 70B GQA, FFN width, vocab 32000) are taken from publicly known configs; no config or weight file exists in this sandbox to verify them.\", \"Quantization uses group size 128 with FP16 scales/zero-points (giving ~0.53 bytes/weight), embeddings and lm_head stay FP16, and calibration uses an OpenAI-style C4 subset of 128x2048 with all scale-fitting performed offline and excluded from timing.\", \"The QuaRot-style implementation exposes independent toggles for offline rotations, the down_proj online Hadamard, KV-cache Hadamard and KV quantization, and permits an identity-rotation pass-through path for the harness-overhead control.\", \"The KV-cache Hadamard refresh policy (how often and over what span the cache is re-rotated) is a parameter, not a known constant; the long-context ladder is designed to locate the crossover rather than assume one.\", \"Attention backend is held constant across arms, with dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant treated as an explicit factor.\", \"Runtime-efficiency figures in the resource budget assume 40% MFU; the estimate scales linearly with the true value.\", \"At least 2x80GB GPUs are available only for the optional 70B arm; the primary study is single-GPU.\", \"Quality-neutral is operationally defined by the pre-registered band: mean delta WikiText-2 PPL <= 0.10, delta C4 <= 0.15, no seed above 0.25.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the available INT4 GEMM kernel realizes anywhere near its ISA-expected throughput at prefill shapes; if it does not, prefill results are lower bounds and must be attributed to kernel maturity rather than to the quantization scheme.\", \"The sequence-length crossover at which online/periodic Hadamard cost cancels the 4-bit decode gain; the analytic estimate places it near S~4k for 7B but the true location depends on the implementation's refresh policy and fusion.\", \"Whether the activation-quantization step (A1 -> A2 on the decode ladder) is net-neutral or net-negative at batch 1; the byte analysis says no saving, the overhead is unquantified.\", \"Whether 4-bit KV degrades quality differently across model sizes, and whether the KV speed benefit at 70B is genuinely inert due to GQA.\", \"Actual shipped-kernel fusion state, rotation construction details, group size and calibration settings of the QuaRot code - unverifiable without the repository, hence parameterized.\", \"True MFU for both FP16 and INT4 paths, and the real wall-clock cost of 70B tensor-parallel sharding, which is excluded from the budget estimate.\", \"Whether the published QuaRot latency table is comparable to this harness at all (different serving stack, clocks and kernels); it is used only as a gross-consistency anchor.\", \"No measurement of any kind is possible in this sandbox, so every quantitative statement in the design remains analytic or placeholder until executed elsewhere.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-fb3ec589-674b-4824-87c4-a4fb7f6989db", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {\"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# QuaRot W4A4 on LLaMA-2 \\u2014 Experiment Design (prefill vs decode)\\n\\n**Status:** design-only. Nothing here is a measured result. All quantitative values below are either **analytic arithmetic** (computed in this sandbox, formulas shown) or **placeholders**. Facts that could not be verified in this environment are tagged **[ASSUMPTION]**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Scope fixed by researcher approval\\n\\n| Item | Fixed decision |\\n|---|---|\\n| Hardware | **A100-80GB**, 1 GPU for 7B/13B. **H100 parameterized as the \\\"no-INT4-tensor-core\\\" alternative condition** `[A_alt]`; prefill conclusions are hardware-pinned and never pooled across the two. |\\n| Models | LLaMA-2 **7B + 13B primary**; **70B optional**, needs \\u22652\\u00d780 GB, with a pre-registered drop rule (\\u00a78). |\\n| Quality | **Perplexity primary** (WikiText-2 len-512, C4 subset); zero-shot battery = secondary/diagnostic. Pre-registered \\u0394PPL band defines \\\"quality-neutral\\\". |\\n| Headline | **Two regime-specific speedups**: decode = batch-1 per-token latency; prefill = large-batch (S\\u22651024, B\\u22658) tokens/s + TTFT. **No single \\\"average speedup\\\" is ever reported.** |\\n| Published QuaRot numbers | Sanity **reference anchor only**, separate table, explicitly labelled *externally reported*, never inside our CIs. |\\n| CPU dry run | Included; **arithmetic only** (FLOP/byte accounting, roofline regime map, bytes-moved/token, KV-bytes/token). No latency/PPL claim from it. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Rationale \\u2014 the causal model the design tests\\n\\nTwo different bottlenecks are in play, so a single scalar answer is meaningless:\\n\\n- **Decode at B=1** is **memory-bandwidth-bound**. The only thing that helps is moving fewer bytes: weights (2 B \\u2192 ~0.53 B with group-128 scales) and KV cache (2 B \\u2192 0.5 B). Ceiling \\u2248 weight/KV byte-compression ratio, **independent of INT4 tensor cores**. QuaRot adds *new* per-token work (activation quantization, online Hadamards) that is FLOP-cheap but **traffic- and launch-heavy** \\u2192 can eat the gain.\\n- **Prefill at S\\u00b7B \\u2265 1024** is **compute-bound**. Gain ceiling = (INT4-path peak TOPS)/(FP16 peak), i.e. **hardware-pinned**: ~4\\u00d7 on A100 (INT4 TC) but only ~2\\u00d7 on H100 (no INT4 TC \\u2192 INT8-pair path) **[ASSUMPTION: datasheet specs]**. Plus per-layer quantize/rotate overhead that FP16 does not pay.\\n\\nSo the design's central object is **speedup as a function of arithmetic intensity**, and the causal question is *which component (weight bytes, KV bytes, GEMM ISA, rotation, quantization) supplies the gain or the cost in each regime*.\\n\\n**Analytic regime justification (computed here, A100 datasheet values [ASSUMPTION]):** memory\\u2192compute crossover token-count M\\\\* = (peak/bandwidth)\\u00b7bytes_per_elem/2 \\u2192 **FP16 M\\\\* \\u2248 153 tokens, W4 M\\\\* \\u2248 81 tokens**. Decode at B \\u2264 64 sits far below \\u2192 bandwidth-bound \\u2714. Prefill at S\\u00b7B \\u2265 1024 sits far above \\u2192 compute-bound \\u2714. These definitions are therefore not arbitrary.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Arms, each tied to a hypothesis with a pre-written expected direction\\n\\nBaseline for all speedups is **A0**. Every arm uses the **same harness, same attention backend, same sampling, same clocks**.\\n\\n| Arm | Config | Hypothesis tested | Expected direction \\u2014 prefill (compute-bound) | Expected direction \\u2014 decode B=1 (BW-bound) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **A0** | BF16/FP16, FP16 KV | Reference; establishes harness overhead \\u2248 0 | 1.00 by definition | 1.00 |\\n| **A1** | W4A16 weight-only (AWQ-style), FP16 KV | How much of the win is *free* from shrinking weights alone, without activation quant | \\u2248 **0.9\\u20131.0\\u00d7** (bandwidth win is off the table; GEMM dequant overhead may hurt) | **\\u2248 3.5\\u20133.8\\u00d7** ceiling (weight bytes dominate: analytic KV share at B=1 is 0.4\\u20130.5 % at 7B/13B) |\\n| **A2** | QuaRot W4A4, **KV16** | Does activation quant + rotation help/hurt once weights are already 4-bit? | \\u2265 A1; tests whether INT4 GEMM actually uses the ISA | vs A1 \\u2248 neutral-to-worse (extra quant/rotate traffic, no byte saving) |\\n| **A3** | **QuaRot W4A4 + 4-bit KV (full paper config)** | The headline end-to-end claim | \\u2248 A2 (KV irrelevant at prefill-time compute) | **\\u2265 A2**, largest where KV share is big; at 7B B=64 S=4096 KV is **91 %** of traffic (analytic) vs only **0.9 %** at 70B \\u2192 arm is strongly model/shape-dependent |\\n| **A4** | A3 **minus online Hadamard on `down_proj`** | Isolates the online-Hadamard cost (and its quality necessity) | Cost should be small in FLOPs (analytic: **0.04\\u20130.07 % of layer FLOP**) \\u2192 if A4 \\u226b A3, the cost is *plumbing*, not arithmetic | Same; expect decode-visible gap only via extra unfused kernels/traffic |\\n| **A5** | A3 with **KV dequant but no KV quant** (KV16 = A2) / and KV-rotate off | Isolates KV-quantization from activation-quantization | ~0 effect expected | Positive effect \\u221d KV share \\u21d2 **large at 7B/13B, near-inert at 70B (GQA)** \\u2014 a built-in falsifiable contrast |\\n| **A6** | **Offline-rotations only** (no online transforms, naive quantization) | Shows why online Hadamards exist | \\u2248 A3 in speed | \\u2248 A3 in speed, but **quality collapse expected** \\u2192 the quality half of A3's value |\\n| **A7** | W8A8 INT (optional ceiling) | Where does the quality/speed frontier bend vs a \\\"safe\\\" 8-bit scheme | between A0 and A3 | between A0 and A1 |\\n| **A3-H** | A3 on H100 `[A_alt]` | ISA-pinning control: prefill gain must shrink (~2\\u00d7 ceiling) while **decode B=1 speedup should be nearly unchanged** | predicted \\u2193 vs A100 | predicted \\u2248 unchanged |\\n\\n**Key design property:** A1\\u2192A2\\u2192A3 is an additive ladder (weights \\u2192 activations \\u2192 KV), so the decode speedup decomposes into weight-byte, activation-overhead and KV-byte contributions; A4/A6 vs A3 attributes the rotation machinery; A3 vs A3-H attributes the ISA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Non-arm controls (first-class, not footnotes)\\n\\n- **C1 \\u2014 Matched-shape GEMM microbenchmark (kernel-efficiency control).** For every linear projection shape (h\\u00d7h, h\\u00d7ffn, ffn\\u00d7h) in each model, run FP16 vs INT4 GEMM at prefill-M (B\\u00b7S \\u2208 {1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}) and decode-M (B \\u2208 {1,8,32,64}), with and without the fused dequant/quant epilogue. This yields an **achievable-kernel-efficiency ratio** that is the yardstick for judging end-to-end prefill. It separates *\\\"quantization is bad\\\"* from *\\\"this INT4 GEMM kernel is immature.\\\"*\\n- **C2 \\u2014 Rotation plumbing toggle.** fused vs unfused application of every online Hadamard; also block-size sweep. Report **online-Hadamard time as % of step time**, never averaged away.\\n- **C3 \\u2014 Hadamard arithmetic-only microbench** (square matrices, sizes matching h and ffn) to price the transform independently of the model.\\n- **C4 \\u2014 Long-context ladder, reported as marginal cost.** S \\u2208 {128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, 16384, 32768}: report **d(latency)/dS for A3 minus A0** as its own curve; **do not fold into a mean**. Analytic prediction to test: a *periodic full-cache* Hadamard refresh costs S\\u00b7h\\u00b7log\\u2082h per layer, which equals one 7B decode step around **S \\u2248 4k** and \\u2248 8\\u00d7 at S = 32k \\u2192 the crossover is expected inside the ladder, and its location is the finding. (Refresh policy itself is **[ASSUMPTION]**; parameterized as \\\"every N tokens\\\".)\\n- **C5 \\u2014 Identical-execution-state controls.** CUDA-graph on/off and `torch.compile` state **identical across all arms** (primary: CUDA-graphs on; secondary repeat with graphs off \\u2014 the *difference* is reported as launch-overhead sensitivity). Analytic launch sensitivity computed here: 2\\u20134 extra kernels/layer \\u00d7 ~5 \\u00b5s = **0.3\\u20131.6 ms/token** for 7B\\u201370B [ASSUMPTION: launch cost], comparable to an entire batch-1 decode step \\u2192 graphs-off is an informative condition, not noise.\\n- **C6 \\u2014 Clock/environment lock.** `nvidia-smi -lgc` locked clocks, ECC on, MPS off, no co-tenants, warmup \\u2265 10 iters, \\u2265 30 timed iters (prefill) / \\u2265 200 generated tokens \\u00d7 \\u2265 5 repeats (decode), **median + IQR**, 3 independent process restarts per cell, randomized run order (anti-thermal-drift), same GPU PCI-BDF.\\n- **C7 \\u2014 Attention-path control.** A5/KV16 vs A3/4-bit KV changes the attention kernel's byte load *and* possibly its implementation. Require the **same fused attention kernel** in both, with an explicit dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant variant reported separately.\\n- **C8 \\u2014 Seeds.** \\u2265 3 **rotation seeds** \\u00d7 1 calibration seed for the full grid; plus \\u2265 3 **calibration seeds at 7B only** to price calibration variance. Report checkpoint-to-checkpoint spread as an error bar alongside the timing CI, since a rotation-seed-dependent PPL would otherwise masquerade as a scheme effect.\\n- **C9 \\u2014 Harness-overhead control.** A0 run through the QuaRot execution path (rotation matrices set to identity, quantizers set to pass-through). Any residual slowdown is harness tax and is subtracted from every arm's interpretation, reported explicitly rather than silently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Config matrix\\n\\n| Axis | Values |\\n|---|---|\\n| Models | 7B, 13B (primary); 70B (secondary, \\u00a78 rule) |\\n| Arms | A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6 (+A7 optional) \\u00d7 {7B,13B}; A3/A0 only for A3-H on H100 |\\n| Prefill S | 128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096 |\\n| Prefill B | 1, 8, 32, 64 (headline cell: S\\u22651024 \\u2227 B\\u22658) |\\n| Decode prompt / gen | prompt \\u2208 {128, 4096}, gen = 128 (4096-prompt: gen 256) |\\n| Decode B | 1 (headline), 8, 32, 64 |\\n| Quant group size | 128 **[ASSUMPTION \\u2014 repo default unverifiable here]**, sensitivity at 64/128 for A3 at 7B |\\n| Long-context ladder | 128 \\u2192 32768, A3 vs A0 only |\\n| Quality | WikiText-2 (len-512, non-overlapping), C4 subset, plus zero-shot diagnostic set |\\n| Rotation seed | 3 | Calibration seed | 3 (7B only) |\\n| Reps | 3 process restarts per timing cell |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Measurement protocol\\n\\n**Operational definitions (pre-registered):**\\n- **TTFT** = t(sampled first token) \\u2212 t(prefill dispatch), *including* `lm_head` + sampling, *excluding* tokenization/H2D. Reported per (S, B).\\n- **Prefill throughput** = (B\\u00b7S)/(prefill latency), tokens/s.\\n- **Decode per-token latency** = (step time) at steady state after prefill, median over generated tokens (first 5 discarded).\\n- **Memory footprint** = `max_memory_allocated` + reserved, split into **weights / KV cache / activations**, measured per arm; plus the *analytic* bytes-moved/token as a covariate so measured-vs-predicted can be compared.\\n- **Arithmetic intensity** per cell = analytic FLOPs \\u00f7 analytic bytes moved; used as the x-axis of the regime map.\\n\\n**Procedure per cell:** load arm \\u2192 warmup \\u2192 N timed iterations \\u2192 per-op breakdown via profiler (`torch.profiler` + one Nsight Systems trace per representative cell, not per cell) \\u2192 dump per-run CSV with git commit, GPU UUID, clock readings, all seeds. Nothing is aggregated across GPUs or across CUDA-graph states.\\n\\n**Quality protocol:** FP16 reference PPL from the *same* eval script and same tokenization; \\u0394PPL = PPL(arm) \\u2212 PPL(A0), computed **per checkpoint** then summarized. Zero-shot battery reported as \\u0394-accuracy, diagnostic only, never gating.\\n\\n**Pre-registered quality gates (\\\"quality-neutral\\\" operational definition):**\\n- **PASS** if \\u0394PPL(WikiText-2) \\u2264 **+0.10** and \\u0394PPL(C4) \\u2264 **+0.15** (mean over rotation seeds), and no seed exceeds +0.25.\\n- **MARGINAL** if \\u2264 +0.25 \\u2192 speedups reported but explicitly labelled \\\"quality-degraded\\\".\\n- **FAIL** if > +0.25 \\u2192 arm's speedups are reported as *unusable-in-practice*, not as wins.\\n- A speedup is quotable as a **win** only for arms passing PASS.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan\\n\\n**(a) Regime map (headline figure).** x-axis = arithmetic intensity (analytic, log scale), y-axis = speedup vs A0, points = config cells, colour = arm, panels = {7B, 13B, 70B} \\u00d7 {A100, H100}. Expected structure: a plateau \\u2248 byte-compression ratio at low intensity (decode), decaying toward the ISA ceiling at high intensity (prefill), with a knee at M\\\\*. **Findings are reported as the shape of this curve plus the two headline cells, never as an average.**\\n\\n**(b) Additive attribution on the decode ladder.** \\u0394ms/token: A0\\u2192A1 (weight bytes), A1\\u2192A2 (activation quant + rotation), A2\\u2192A3 (KV quant). Each delta carries a CI from seed \\u00d7 restart variance.\\n\\n**(c) Attribution accounting identity (the core anti-confound rule).** For each cell,\\n`T(arm) \\u2212 T(A0) = \\u03a3 \\u0394T_GEMM + T_rotate + T_quant + T_KV + T_resid`,\\nwith `\\u0394T_GEMM` **taken from C1 microbenchmarks, not from the arm itself**. Then:\\n- `|T_resid| \\u2264 20 %` of the gap \\u2192 attribution **accepted**, scheme-level conclusion allowed.\\n- `|T_resid| > 20 %` \\u2192 conclusion is **blocked**; report \\\"unexplained overhead, attribution unresolved\\\".\\n\\n**(d) Explicit decision rules (stated before any measurement).**\\n- **\\\"W4A4 helps decode but not prefill\\\"** is claimable **iff** all of: (i) decode B=1 speedup CI lower bound > 1.05\\u00d7 for A3 on \\u2265 2 of 3 restarts; (ii) the prefill headline cell speedup CI **contains 1.0 or lies below it**; (iii) A3 passes the quality gate; (iv) C1 shows the INT4 GEMM reaching **\\u2265 0.7\\u00d7 of its ISA-expected efficiency** at prefill shapes \\u2014 i.e. the prefill failure is not a broken kernel; (v) the same holds on the pinned hardware, stated separately for A100 and H100.\\n- **\\\"Kernel immaturity, not the quantization scheme\\\"** is the attribution **iff** C1's INT4:FP16 efficiency ratio is **< 0.5\\u00d7** the datasheet-implied ratio **and** the end-to-end prefill shortfall is accounted for within \\u00b120 % by the C1 deficit plus measured rotate/quant time (condition (iv) fails). In that case the paper-facing claim is *\\\"the scheme's prefill potential is not realized by the available kernels\\\"*, and prefill numbers are reported as **lower bounds**.\\n- **\\\"Rotation machinery is affordable\\\"** iff A3 \\u2212 A4 speed gap \\u2264 5 % **and** A3 quality \\u2212 A6 quality gap is in the intended direction (A3 clearly better). If A3 \\u2212 A4 gap > 15 %, the finding is *plumbing-bound*, reported as such.\\n- **\\\"KV quantization is worthwhile\\\"** iff A3 \\u2212 A5 is positive with a CI excluding zero at **both** 7B B=64 S=4096 (high KV share) and B=1 (low share). Predicted-as-inert at 70B is itself a reportable outcome, not a failure.\\n- **Capacity claim (separate from speed):** W4A4 weights for 70B are **39 GB analytic vs 145.5 GB FP16** \\u2192 70B may become *single-GPU-feasible*, which is reported as a footprint result even where speedups are null.\\n\\n**(e) Statistics.** Median + IQR over cells; bootstrap CI over restarts \\u00d7 seeds; **no pooling across hardware**; Holm correction across the arm-comparison family; every figure traceable to a CSV row.\\n\\n**(f) External anchor.** Published QuaRot latency/PPL table reproduced into a clearly-labelled *externally reported* appendix table, used only to flag gross inconsistencies (e.g. > 2\\u00d7 off in the same regime \\u21d2 harness bug hunt), never merged into CIs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. CPU-feasible dry-run \\u2014 scope and results obtained here\\n\\n**Scope (permitted):** arithmetic from LLaMA-2 architecture constants **[ASSUMPTION: taken from publicly known configs; no local config/weights exist here]** and vendor datasheet specs **[ASSUMPTION: no GPU present]** \\u2014 weight/KV footprints, bytes-moved/token, arithmetic-intensity crossovers, overhead-free ceilings, Hadamard marginal-cost ratios, GPU-hour budget. **Nothing here is a latency, throughput or PPL measurement.**\\n\\nComputed outputs (to ship as the design's predicted-regime table):\\n\\n```\\nFootprint (analytic, group-128 scales):        Regime crossover M* (tokens), A100 [datasheet]:\\n 7B  FP16 12.3GB -> W4 3.45GB   KV 512->128 KB/tok   FP16 ~153   W8 ~158   W4 ~81\\n 13B FP16 23.9GB -> W4 6.58GB   KV 800->200 KB/tok   => decode (B<=64) bandwidth-bound;\\n 70B FP16 145.5GB-> W4 39.0GB   KV 320->80  KB/tok      prefill (S*B>=1024) compute-bound\\n\\nDecode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 (analytic, weights+KV):\\n 7B  B=1 S=128: 3.57x (KV share 0.5%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 91.2%)\\n 13B B=1 S=128: 3.64x (KV share 0.4%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.96x (KV share 89.3%)\\n 70B B=1 S=4096:3.73x (KV share 0.9%)   B=64 S=4096: 3.82x (KV share 35.5%)\\n => KV-quant performance effect is large at 7B/13B high-B*S, nearly inert at 70B (GQA)\\n\\nOverhead-free ceilings: A100 compute-bound 4.0x, byte-bound 3.76x | H100 (no INT4 TC) compute-bound 2.0x\\nOnline Hadamard per token/layer: 0.01-0.07% of layer FLOP, 16-112 KB extra RW traffic\\n => cost is plumbing/launch (0.3-1.6 ms/token if unfused), NOT arithmetic\\nFull-KV Hadamard refresh vs one decode step (7B): S=1k 0.25x | S=4k 0.99x | S=16k 3.98x | S=32k 7.96x\\n => long-context ladder must span 128..32768 to capture the crossover\\n```\\n\\n**Explicitly out of scope:** no latency, no tokens/s, no PPL, no accuracy, no \\\"expected speedup we obtained\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Resources / runtime estimate \\u2014 **estimates, marked as such**\\n\\nAnalytic FLOP-based budget, A100 @ 40 % assumed MFU **[ASSUMPTION: MFU unknown; scales linearly with it]**, 7 arms, 3 seeds, per \\u00a74 grid:\\n\\n| Model | perf sweeps | offline quant | PPL eval | total (GPU\\u00b7h) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 7B | ~3.2 | ~0.05 | ~0.4 | **~3.7** |\\n| 13B | ~6.3 | ~0.1 | ~0.8 | **~7.2** |\\n| 70B (2-GPU, sharding overhead not included) | ~38.3 | ~0.6 | ~5.1 | **~44+** |\\n\\nPlus microbenchmarks C1\\u2013C3 (~1\\u20132 GPU\\u00b7h) and one H100 A3/A0 repeat (~2 GPU\\u00b7h on H100). **Total \\u2248 15 GPU\\u00b7h for the primary 7B+13B study; 70B adds ~3\\u00d7 that**, i.e. 70B is ~75 % of total cost for 2 of ~10 reportable contrasts.\\n\\n**Pre-registered 70B drop rule:** run 70B only if (i) \\u2265 2\\u00d780 GB are actually available, (ii) projected 70B cost \\u2264 40 % of the approved GPU-hour budget, and (iii) the sharded harness reproduces 7B A0 timings within 5 % of the single-GPU case (harness sanity). If any fails, **drop 70B to quality-only** (A0/A3 \\u0394PPL + zero-shot, no timing) and say so in the limitations; do not silently shrink the primary grid.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Risks / limitations\\n\\n1. **Kernel maturity dominates the prefill conclusion.** Mitigated by C1/C9 and the \\u00b120 % residual rule, but never eliminated; prefill numbers may only be lower bounds.\\n2. **Hardware ISA is a design axis, not a detail.** On H100 the prefill ceiling roughly halves while the decode ceiling is unchanged \\u2014 pooling would be a category error.\\n3. **Rotation/Hadamard fusion state of the shipped code is unknown [ASSUMPTION]** \\u2192 C2/C5 treat it as an experimental factor rather than a fixed property.\\n4. **70B GQA makes KV ablations inert**; a null A3-vs-A5 there must be pre-declared as *expected*, else it reads as a contradiction of the 7B result.\\n5. **KV dequant may force a different attention kernel**, which alone can move decode latency (C7).\\n6. **Per-token overhead is launch-bound at batch 1** \\u2192 CUDA-graph state changes the answer; both states reported.\\n7. **Calibration/rotation seed variance** may exceed the arm effect for quality; hence C8 and per-checkpoint \\u0394PPL.\\n8. **PPL-insensitive damage** (long-context, code, reasoning) is out of scope; zero-shot battery is diagnostic only.\\n9. **Non-determinism / thermal drift** handled by clocks-lock + randomized order, not by more averaging.\\n10. **No verification of the repository is possible in this environment** \\u2014 every repo-specific setting (group size, rotation construction, calibration set, kernel flags, whether `lm_head`/embeddings stay FP16) is a parameterized assumption, not a fact.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Deliverable artifacts (to be produced when compute exists)\\n\\nconfig matrix YAML \\u00b7 arm builder (identity-rotation pass-through path for C9) \\u00b7 timing harness with per-op profiler hooks \\u00b7 C1 microbench suite \\u00b7 \\u0394PPL/eval script \\u00b7 attribution-residual analysis script \\u2192 regime-map figure + ladder-decomposition table + appendix external-anchor table \\u00b7 per-run CSV archive with environment provenance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Assumptions vs verified facts (final separation)\\n\\n**Verified here:** `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (no QuaRot repo, no weights/config, no harness, no kernels, no calibration data); network dead (DNS failure for pypi + github); Python 3.14.4 with **no** torch/transformers/numpy/etc.; no GPU device (`nvidia-smi` cannot reach driver, no `/dev/nvidia*`); 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, gcc-15/git present. The \\u00a77 numbers are arithmetic I ran in this sandbox.\\n\\n**Assumed, not known:** A100-80GB/H100 datasheet bandwidth & TOPS; LLaMA-2 config constants; group size 128; OpenAI-style C4 calibration set (128\\u00d72048); offline scale-fitting excluded from timing; refresh policy for KV Hadamards; fusion status of shipped kernels; 40 % MFU for the budget; embeddings/`lm_head` kept in FP16; \\u22652 GPUs for 70B.\", \"evidence\": \"ENVIRONMENT (executed in sandbox, all successful = not proof of any experiment): `ls -la /workspace` -> only task.json (405 B); `du -sh /workspace` -> 8.0K; `cat /workspace/task.json` -> \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"; `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*quarot*'` -> empty; `~/.cache` absent, no /opt, no *.whl, no libtorch -> no repo, weights, HF configs, harness, CUDA kernels or calibration data obtainable; network dead (urllib to pypi.org and `git ls-remote github.com/spcl/QuaRot` both -> DNS resolution failure); python 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3, pip 25.1.1, find_spec probe shows torch/transformers/numpy/datasets/accelerate/sentencepiece/tokenizers/scipy/pandas/triton/lm_eval all False; `nvidia-smi` -> \\\"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\", no /dev/nvidia* (CUDA 13.1 toolkit + libcuda.so.595.71.05 stub present but no device); nproc=224, MemTotal ~2.1 TB, Xeon Platinum 8570, gcc-15 + git available, /tmp tmpfs 1 TB. ANALYTIC CPU DRY RUN (pure arithmetic, three scripts run with python3, no GPU/latency/PPL involved): weight footprints 7B 12.3->3.45 GB, 13B 23.9->6.58 GB, 70B 145.5->39.0 GB (group-128 scales); KV bytes/token 7B 512->128 KB, 13B 800->200 KB, 70B 320->80 KB; roofline crossover M* 153 (FP16) vs 81 (W4) tokens on A100 datasheet specs -> justifies bandwidth-bound decode (B<=64) vs compute-bound prefill (S*B>=1024); decode traffic ratio FP16/W4A4 3.57-3.96x with KV share 0.4-0.5% at B=1 but 89-91% at B=64,S=4096 for 7B/13B vs only 0.9-35.5% for 70B (GQA) -> predicts A5 (KV ablation) is shape- and model-dependent and near-inert at 70B; overhead-free ceilings 4.0x compute-bound on A100 vs 2.0x on H100 (no INT4 tensor cores), 3.76x weight-byte-bound; online-Hadamard marginal cost 0.011-0.073% of layer FLOP and 16-112 KB/token extra traffic but 0.32-1.6 ms/token if it costs 2-4 unfused kernel launches per layer at ~5 us -> cost is plumbing, not arithmetic; full-KV Hadamard refresh equals one 7B decode step at S~4k and 8x at S=32k -> motivates the 128..32768 ladder; GPU-hour budget ~3.7 (7B), ~7.2 (13B), ~44+ (70B) at an assumed 40% MFU -> 70B is ~75% of cost, which grounds the pre-registered drop rule. NO measured latency, throughput, memory-occupancy or perplexity value was produced or is claimed anywhere.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Hardware is A100-80GB with ~2.04 TB/s HBM and ~312 TFLOPS FP16 / INT4 tensor cores, and H100 as the parameterized no-INT4-TC alternative (~3.35 TB/s, ~989 TFLOPS FP16, INT8-pair INT4 path); all such values are vendor datasheet figures, unmeasurable here.\", \"LLaMA-2 7B/13B/70B architectural constants (hidden size, layers, head counts incl. 70B GQA, FFN width, vocab 32000) are taken from publicly known configs; no config or weight file exists in this sandbox to verify them.\", \"Quantization uses group size 128 with FP16 scales/zero-points (giving ~0.53 bytes/weight), embeddings and lm_head stay FP16, and calibration uses an OpenAI-style C4 subset of 128x2048 with all scale-fitting performed offline and excluded from timing.\", \"The QuaRot-style implementation exposes independent toggles for offline rotations, the down_proj online Hadamard, KV-cache Hadamard and KV quantization, and permits an identity-rotation pass-through path for the harness-overhead control.\", \"The KV-cache Hadamard refresh policy (how often and over what span the cache is re-rotated) is a parameter, not a known constant; the long-context ladder is designed to locate the crossover rather than assume one.\", \"Attention backend is held constant across arms, with dequant-in-attention vs separate-dequant treated as an explicit factor.\", \"Runtime-efficiency figures in the resource budget assume 40% MFU; the estimate scales linearly with the true value.\", \"At least 2x80GB GPUs are available only for the optional 70B arm; the primary study is single-GPU.\", \"Quality-neutral is operationally defined by the pre-registered band: mean delta WikiText-2 PPL <= 0.10, delta C4 <= 0.15, no seed above 0.25.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the available INT4 GEMM kernel realizes anywhere near its ISA-expected throughput at prefill shapes; if it does not, prefill results are lower bounds and must be attributed to kernel maturity rather than to the quantization scheme.\", \"The sequence-length crossover at which online/periodic Hadamard cost cancels the 4-bit decode gain; the analytic estimate places it near S~4k for 7B but the true location depends on the implementation's refresh policy and fusion.\", \"Whether the activation-quantization step (A1 -> A2 on the decode ladder) is net-neutral or net-negative at batch 1; the byte analysis says no saving, the overhead is unquantified.\", \"Whether 4-bit KV degrades quality differently across model sizes, and whether the KV speed benefit at 70B is genuinely inert due to GQA.\", \"Actual shipped-kernel fusion state, rotation construction details, group size and calibration settings of the QuaRot code - unverifiable without the repository, hence parameterized.\", \"True MFU for both FP16 and INT4 paths, and the real wall-clock cost of 70B tensor-parallel sharding, which is excluded from the budget estimate.\", \"Whether the published QuaRot latency table is comparable to this harness at all (different serving stack, clocks and kernels); it is used only as a gross-consistency anchor.\", \"No measurement of any kind is possible in this sandbox, so every quantitative statement in the design remains analytic or placeholder until executed elsewhere.\"]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
